\\", \\\"esa/sandbox-mirror\\\"]\\n- [\\\"git\\\", \\\"rebase\\\", \\\"--show-current-patch\\\"]\\n- [\\\"rm\\\", \\\"-rf\\\", \\\"/tmp/renoise-space-smoke\\\"]\\n- [\\\"git\\\", \\\"update-ref\\\", \\\"refs/heads/master\\\"]\\n- [\\\"git\\\", \\\"-C\\\", \\\"/Users/esaruoho/work/apple\\\"]\\n- [\\\"ssh\\\", \\\"100.117.30.102\\\", \\\"hostname; date\\\"]\\n- [\\\"git\\\", \\\"restore\\\", \\\"--pathspec-from-file=-\\\"]...\\n[Some commands were truncated]\\n The writable roots are `/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var/folders/ly/fy8lysdn46x8hx7ybrhcmyf80000gn/T`.\\n</permissions instructions>\"},{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"<collaboration_mode># Collaboration Mode: Default\\n\\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\\n\\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\\n\\n## request_user_input availability\\n\\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\\n\\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\\n\\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\\n\\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\\n\\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\\n\\nIf explicit user input is required for another reason before progress can safely continue, do not use the `request_user_input` tool. Ask the user directly with one concise plain-text question instead. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\\n</collaboration_mode>\"},{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"<apps_instructions>\\n## Apps (Connectors)\\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\\n</apps_instructions>\"},{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"<plugins_instructions>\\n## Plugins\\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps.\\n### How to use plugins\\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\\n- MCP naming: Plugin-provided MCP tools keep standard MCP identifiers such as `mcp__server__tool`; use tool provenance to tell which plugin they come from.\\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\\n- Relevance: Determine what a plugin can help with from explicit user mention or from the plugin-associated skills, MCP tools, and apps exposed elsewhere in this turn.\\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\\n</plugins_instructions>\"}],\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a0672e-d148-7693-87c0-390d6f9165f4\",\"create_time\":1788437517.131568,\"content_item_kinds\":[\"host_skills.instructions\",\"permissions.instructions\",\"collaboration_mode.instructions\",\"apps.instructions\",\"plugins.usage_instructions\"]}}}\n{\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-03T12:11:57.132Z\",\"ordinal\":3,\"type\":\"response_item\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"message\",\"id\":\"msg_01a0672e-d34b-7813-bb5e-6a0ff5a147a4\",\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"# AGENTS.md instructions for /Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx\\n\\n<INSTRUCTIONS>\\n# Global Instructions\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: COPY-TO-CLIPBOARD ALWAYS USES RICH TEXT WHEN THERE IS RICH TEXT — NEVER LEAVE MARKDOWN JUNK ON THE PASTEBOARD\\n\\n**When Esa asks \\\"copy X to clipboard\\\" and X contains ANY formatting that would render as rich text (bold `**`, italic `*` / `_`, headings `#`, lists `-` / `*` / `1.`, blockquotes `>`, links `[text](url)`, code spans `` ` ``, code fences ```` ``` ````), the clipboard payload MUST be RTF — never raw markdown, never plain-text with stripped markdown.**\\n\\nApple-native pipeline is already built and working: `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/md-to-clipboard <file.md>` (also exposed as the `/md-rich` slash command). Renders Markdown → HTML → RTF → pasteboard via `python3 + textutil + osascript`. No Homebrew, no pip. Skill reference: `~/.Codex/skills/rich-text-clipboard/SKILL.md`.\\n\\n### The rule — decision tree\\n\\nBefore any clipboard operation, walk this tree:\\n\\n1. **Does the source have any formatting** (bold `**`, italic, headings, lists, links, code spans)? If no → plain `pbcopy`. Done.\\n2. **Source has formatting. What's the paste target?**\\n   - **Rich-text-capable** (Mail.app, Apple Notes, Pages, TextEdit, Word, Outlook, Messages-to-iOS) → **RTF via `/md-rich` / `md-to-clipboard`**. This is the default for outgoing emails to collaborators.\\n   - **Markdown-flavored** (Discord, Slack, GitHub issue/PR, Reddit) → **plain `pbcopy` with markdown intact**. The target renders its own markdown flavor; RTF paste gets mangled. Slack uses single-asterisk `*bold*`, Discord uses GitHub-flavored markdown.\\n   - **Plain-text-only** (SMS to non-iOS, terminal/shell, `.txt` files, code-editor buffers) → **plain `pbcopy` with markdown intact**. The user will see the raw `**` markers, which is the correct behavior because there's no rendering anywhere downstream.\\n3. **NEVER strip markdown to plain text as a workaround.** Esa wrote: *\\\"can we make it a Codex global rule that copy to clipboard always uses richtext, if theres richtext. not 'markdown' junk.\\\"* The old \\\"strip `**` and `#` before `pbcopy`\\\" workaround is obsolete — `/md-rich` exists, works, and is the right answer for rich-text-capable targets. Stripping loses information and is never the right move.\\n4. **If unsure which category the target is in:** ask Esa, or default to RTF if the destination is \\\"an email\\\" / \\\"a document\\\" / \\\"a note\\\" (almost always rich-text-capable). Only default to plain markdown if Esa explicitly named Discord, Slack, GitHub, or a chat app.\\n\\n**Byte-count sanity check:** after `md-to-clipboard`, confirm `«class RTF », N` where N > 500 for non-trivial input. The script reports the count itself. Anything ≪ 1000 bytes for a multi-paragraph source means the pipeline broke (the prior 2026-05-26 incident produced a 144-byte empty stub due to a heredoc-vs-pipe bug — now fixed, but watch for it).\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\nOn 2026-05-26 Esa spent significant time hand-converting `**bold**` markers into actual bold in his Mail.app composer because I shipped markdown-stripped plain text to his clipboard for an outgoing email to Norman Wootan. This was the second incident — the first was 2026-05-08 with a Pitkänen Finnish-questions clipboard payload where the workaround at the time was \\\"strip markdown to plain text.\\\" The proper fix is RTF, not stripping. The `/md-rich` skill existed; I didn't reach for it. The skill's own underlying script had a heredoc-vs-pipe stdin contention bug that produced a 144-byte empty-stub RTF — fixed 2026-05-26 by switching to a tempfile + env var to feed the python heredoc-script.\\n\\n**Diagnostic checklist if rich-text clipboard appears not to work:**\\n- Byte count from `md-to-clipboard` < 500 → pipeline broke; re-check input file is non-empty and `textutil` succeeded.\\n- `osascript -e 'clipboard info'` should show `«class RTF », N` for some N matching the script's reported size.\\n- If paste target shows literal `**` or `#` → either the source app doesn't accept rich text (rare), or the wrong tool was used. Re-paste via `pbpaste | textutil -convert html -stdin -stdout` to inspect.\\n\\n### Companion supersession\\n\\nThis rule **supersedes** the older project-level memory `feedback_clipboard_outgoing_email_plain_text.md` which said \\\"strip markdown to plain text for clipboard.\\\" That memory was a 2026-05-08 workaround written before `/md-rich` was known to me. RTF is the correct answer; stripping is not.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: RTFC — Rich Text For Clipboard, the right way every time\\n\\n**When Esa types \\\"RTFC\\\" (any case), or says \\\"RTF to clipboard\\\", \\\"rich text clipboard\\\", \\\"copy as RTF\\\", or any near-variant, the most recently-produced relevant content goes onto the macOS clipboard as clean RTF — with NO YAML frontmatter, NO duplicate H1 title, and NO leftover markdown markers. Use the `rtfc` wrapper, not `md-to-clipboard` directly, and not `pbcopy` on raw markdown.**\\n\\n### The command\\n\\n```bash\\n/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/rtfc /path/to/file.md\\n```\\n\\nIt strips YAML frontmatter, strips the leading H1, and pipes the cleaned body to `md-to-clipboard`. Reports `«class RTF », N` byte count.\\n\\n### Response protocol when Esa types RTFC\\n\\n1. **Identify the target file** — default to the most recent markdown file produced this turn. If multiple candidates exist, ASK which one rather than guessing.\\n2. **Run `rtfc <path>`** — never bypass it with `md-to-clipboard` or `pbcopy` on the raw markdown.\\n3. **Confirm** — print the byte count and name the file. Anything below ~500 bytes for a multi-paragraph source = pipeline broke.\\n4. **If Esa flags the result as junk** — do NOT silently retry the same broken pipeline. Diagnose the actual leak (frontmatter? duplicate H1? wrong file?) and fix the root cause.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\n2026-05-27: Esa asked me to copy the cold-fusion ELI3 explainer for Ruby Carat as RTF. I ran `md-to-clipboard` directly on the archive markdown file. The YAML frontmatter (title / date / type / audience / framework / related / status) landed as literal RTF text on the pasteboard. Esa pasted into the message to Ruby, saw the junk, called it out: *\\\"the RichTextFormatting must not have markdown junk like that. it needs to be the communication, that i can send out. do a good work.\\\"*\\n\\nThe `rich-text-clipboard` skill existed. `md-to-clipboard` existed. What did NOT exist was a wrapper that handled the archive-file shape (frontmatter + H1 title) reliably. The `rtfc` script fills that gap. From now on RTFC is the user-facing command, and behind it the wrapper does the cleaning correctly every time.\\n\\n### Companion rules\\n\\n- Skill rule: `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/rtfc-clipboard.md` (full detail, established same session)\\n- Underlying skill: `~/.Codex/skills/rich-text-clipboard/SKILL.md`\\n- Wrapper script: `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/rtfc`\\n- Parent rule above: § \\\"MANDATORY: COPY-TO-CLIPBOARD ALWAYS USES RICH TEXT WHEN THERE IS RICH TEXT\\\" — RTFC is the operational tool that makes the parent rule work for archive markdown files. Parent said WHAT; RTFC is HOW.\\n\\n### Single-sentence statement\\n\\nWhen Esa types RTFC, run `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/rtfc <path>`, confirm `«class RTF », N`, and name the file in the response — no frontmatter, no duplicate H1, no markdown markers, ever.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: SYNCTHING IS THE DEFAULT CHANNEL FOR CLOUDCITY WORK — SSH IS OPPORTUNISTIC, NOT ASSUMED\\n\\n**Anytime work involves the Cloudcity Mac Mini (cloudcity), Syncthing-mediated channels are the default. SSH is a fallback used only when nothing else can do the job, and only after verifying it's actually reachable from this session.**\\n\\n**Robust wins over brittle.** Esa's exact words (2026-05-26 session, after I had spent half a session defaulting to SSH and accumulating zombie ssh/scp processes when mDNS broke): *\\\"I have no access to when you have the access you're allowed to use it, but you cannot expect that the access is already available. The preparation is to always use syncthing because robust wins over brittle.\\\"*\\n\\n### The trap I keep falling into\\n\\nI assume `ssh cloudcity '<cmd>'` is a free, reliable tool and reach for it as the default for: deploying scripts (scp), running shell commands, inspecting logs, killing processes. When mDNS hostname resolution fails OR my non-GUI shell can't reach the macOS Keychain OR the network blips, I retry SSH instead of falling back to Syncthing. Then I queue more SSH attempts in parallel hoping one will succeed. Each leaves a zombie process burning battery on a connect-retry loop. (2026-05-26: 5 stale ssh/scp PIDs, 53min+ uptime each.)\\n\\n### Correct channel hierarchy (use in this order — full detail in `~/.Codex/skills/cloudcity/skill.md`)\\n\\n1. **Syncthing file drop** — write into `~/work/comms/queue/<service>-inbox/`. Mini sees it in seconds. Used for `!pk` commands (`pakettibot-inbox/`), OCR (`ocr-inbox/`), TTS (`voicebox-inbox/`), shell exec via `run --direct`.\\n2. **Git push → auto-pull** — for code deploys. `repo-puller.sh` on the Mini polls every 60s and pulls each tracked repo (`comms`, `whisp-transcripts`, `pakettibot-agent`) with deferred-restart safety so in-flight work isn't destroyed.\\n3. **Syncthing-mirrored READ** — bot logs at `~/work/comms/queue/pakettibot-logs/`, per-agent stdouts at `~/work/comms/queue/pakettibot-agents/`, heartbeats at `~/work/comms/queue/*-heartbeat.json`. **All locally readable, no SSH.**\\n4. **`!pk run --direct --timeout N <cmd>`** — bash exec via file bridge. The `--direct` flag is critical: default `!pk run` goes through iterm-shell-worker which can hang/queue/time out. `--direct` spawns bash inline from the node bot. Use `--direct` for git pull, pkill, ls, cat, find. Use default `!pk run` only for commands that need Automation TCC (screencapture, System Events).\\n5. **SSH** — last resort. Must preflight with `mini-up && timeout 8 ssh -o ConnectTimeout=5 -o BatchMode=yes cloudcity 'true'`. If either fails: STOP, fall back to the channels above. Never retry SSH in a loop.\\n\\n### Pre-action checklist before ANY ssh/scp to the Mini\\n\\n1. Is what I want already possible via Syncthing? (file drop, `!pk run --direct`, git push, reading a synced log) → DO THAT INSTEAD.\\n2. If genuinely SSH-only (sudo, interactive, debugging the bot itself): run `mini-up || abort`, then `timeout 8 ssh -o BatchMode=yes ... 'true' || abort`, then proceed with absolute paths.\\n3. After any backgrounded ssh/scp: `ps -eo pid,etime,command | grep -E \\\"ssh.*CloudcityMacMini|scp.*CloudcityMacMini\\\" | grep -v grep`. Kill orphans.\\n4. Two consecutive SSH failures with hostname-resolution or connection errors → STOP. Switch channel. Don't fight mDNS.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\nHalf of my 2026-05-26 session burned cycles re-learning this. I had built syncthing-mirrored heartbeats but kept reaching for `scp` to deploy scripts. I diagnosed CPU starvation as auth issues. I queued multiple SSH commands hoping one would succeed. Esa was visibly furious — and rightly so. The architecture FOR Syncthing-default already existed; I just kept ignoring it. **This is not optional. SSH is brittle infrastructure. Syncthing is durable infrastructure. Use the durable one by default.**\\n\\nFull skill reference: `~/.Codex/skills/cloudcity/skill.md` (the \\\"Syncthing is the default channel\\\" section at the top).\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: NEVER RUN `git clean` ON `~/work/comms`, `~/work/apple`, OR `~/work/merlib-mirror` — IT CLOBBERS LIVE WORK ACROSS EVERY SYNCTHING PEER\\n\\n**`~/work/comms` is BOTH a git repo AND a Syncthing share. `git clean -fdx` / `git clean -fd` deletes every untracked + ignored file — which is exactly where the live OCR pipeline keeps its work products (`queue/ocr-inbox/`, `queue/ocr-results/`, the `.pdfws` + `.txt` + `_ocr.pdf` trios). Syncthing then propagates those deletions to the Mac Mini and every other peer, permanently. NEVER run `git clean` in comms, in any form, for any reason.**\\n\\n### The rule — absolute\\n\\n- **NEVER** run `git clean -fdx`, `git clean -fd`, `git clean -fx`, `git clean -f`, or any `git clean` variant inside these directories or any subdirectory of them:\\n  - `/Users/esaruoho/work/comms`\\n  - `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple`\\n  - `/Users/esaruoho/work/merlib-mirror`\\n- This extends to **any** directory that is simultaneously a git repo and a Syncthing folder (check for both `.git/` and `.stfolder`/`.stignore` before ever cleaning). When in doubt, do NOT clean — use targeted `rm` of specific named files instead.\\n- Do not run `git clean` as part of a \\\"reset the repo\\\" / \\\"clean the working tree\\\" routine in any of these. If the working tree needs tidying, move untracked files aside with `mv`, never bulk-delete.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\n2026-06-02: ~5 days of OCR work on the VRIL Compendium (Gerry Vassilatos, vols 2–9) was **wiped** when a `git clean -fdx` ran in `~/work/comms`. The OCR outputs were `.gitignore`d, so `git clean` deleted them; Syncthing then propagated the deletion to the Mini and every peer; a `.stignore`/`.gitignore` clash meant there was no `.stversions` history to recover from. The work was only partially recoverable because `~/work/mediabank/` is a **separate git repo NOT inside the comms Syncthing tree** and still held `source/original.pdf` + `artifacts/ocr/ocr.txt` per asset. Esa was livid; this must never recur.\\n\\nCompanion: project memory `project_ocr_outputs_durable_in_mediabank_not_comms.md` (recovery path + mediabank durability) and the Syncthing **file-versioning** that must stay enabled on the comms folder (staggered/trash-can) so any future deletion is recoverable from `.stversions`.\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: NEVER RUN LOCAL OCR ON ESA'S MACHINE — USE CLOUDCITY SYNCTHING OCR PIPELINE\\n\\n**Esa's project rule (`~/work/merlib-dump/AGENTS.md`): \\\"OCR via Cloudcity Syncthing for image-only / garbled PDFs.\\\"** This rule is mandatory in EVERY context, not just when explicitly invoked.\\n\\n**Never run `ocrmypdf`, `tesseract`, or any local OCR tool on Esa's laptop.** Even for \\\"quick identification probes\\\". Local OCR pins multiple CPU cores (tesseract spawns one process per page concurrently) and contributes to fan/SSD/thermal wear — the same hardware Esa depends on for work.\\n\\n### The trap I keep falling into\\n\\nI see an image-only PDF in lookthrough/, want to identify it before deciding where to archive it, and reach for `ocrmypdf --skip-text` \\\"just to read page 1\\\". Wrong tool. Examples from 2026-05-20 session:\\n- Used local ocrmypdf ~40+ times across batches for identification probes\\n- Started ocrmypdf on a 1.3GB Terman archive.org TIFF scan before checking file size; killed it ~30 seconds in once tesseract was already running 5+ cores\\n\\n### Correct pipeline\\n\\n**The pipeline already exists:** `~/.Codex/skills/bbs/bin/bbs-ocr-submit.sh <file.pdf>` drops the PDF into `~/work/comms/queue/ocr-inbox/` which is Syncthing-mirrored to a remote OCR worker; results return to `~/work/comms/queue/ocr-results/`. Status: `bbs-ocr-submit.sh --status`.\\n\\n**Esa's shortcut**: when Esa says **\\\"CCOCR\\\"** or **\\\"cloudcity OCR\\\"** or **\\\"trigger OCR\\\"** or **\\\"send to OCR\\\"** without specifying tool, he means the Cloudcity Syncthing pipeline via `bbs-ocr-submit.sh`. Always.\\n\\n**For identification BEFORE archiving** (when filename alone is ambiguous):\\n1. Check `pdfinfo` first — file size, page count, creator, creation date often identify the source\\n2. Check filename pattern — `2015.449355.*` = archive.org item ID; `s41467-*` = Nature Communications DOI; `10.1XXX-*` = DOI-stem; `XXXX-YYYY-PB.pdf` = OJS Public-Knowledge journal ID; etc.\\n3. If still ambiguous: file under best-effort name (or `sources/_unidentified-pending-OCR/`), submit to Syncthing, re-identify when results return\\n4. **Never run local OCR as an identification probe**\\n\\n**For archive-worthy image-only PDFs:**\\n1. Archive under best-effort filename with `-image-only-needs-OCR` suffix\\n2. Submit to Syncthing via `bbs-ocr-submit.sh`\\n3. Log in `~/work/merlib-dump/articles/MASTER-OCR-LEDGER.md`\\n4. When OCR result returns, replace the in-archive image-only file with the OCR'd version (preserving folder, removing `-image-only-needs-OCR` suffix from filename)\\n\\n### Pre-action checklist before ANY local OCR command\\n\\nBefore typing `ocrmypdf` or `tesseract` anywhere, ask:\\n1. **Could I identify this file without OCR?** (filename pattern, pdfinfo, page-1 image preview)\\n2. **What's the page count and file size?** Anything >100 pages or >50MB should NEVER be locally OCR'd\\n3. **Is this for archival OCR or just identification?** If archival → Syncthing. If identification → see step 1.\\n4. **Is `bbs-ocr-submit.sh` available?** It always is. Use it.\\n\\n**If you find yourself running ocrmypdf locally for any reason except a one-shot tiny-file identification where Syncthing latency is genuinely unworkable: STOP. You're violating the rule.**\\n\\n**This rule exists because I burned Esa's local CPU multiple times in the 2026-05-20 session on local OCR identification probes that should have gone through Syncthing.** Hardware wear is real and accumulates. Esa has limited patience for me re-learning this lesson.\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: NEVER LAUNCH A REGEX THAT CAN CATASTROPHICALLY BACKTRACK — UGREP-SAFE GREP RULES\\n\\n**On Esa's machine, `grep` is wrapped/aliased to `ugrep`. Ugrep's regex engine is vulnerable to catastrophic backtracking on patterns that contain greedy `.*` followed by literals followed by more `.*`, especially in alternations.**\\n\\n**Real incident (2026-05-19 → 2026-05-20):** I wrote `\\\"Marianna King.*Primary Water\\\\|M.*King.*Primary Water Research\\\"` as one of 31 grep patterns in a for-loop during lookthrough-folder triage. The first 30 patterns finished in seconds; that one ran for **27.6 hours at full CPU**, pinning Esa's laptop fans and thermally cycling the hardware. The harness sent `completed exit code 0` notifications hours after the underlying ugrep was still alive — those notifications were lies and I trusted them. I never ran `ps aux` to check for orphan processes between batches. This caused real hardware wear to Esa's laptop, which he depends on. **He was rightfully furious.**\\n\\n### Banned regex shapes — DO NOT WRITE THESE, EVER\\n\\nThese patterns trigger catastrophic backtracking in ugrep and similar regex engines. Banned in every project, every script, every Bash one-liner, every background task:\\n\\n- `<short-prefix>.*<literal>` where `<short-prefix>` is 1-2 characters — e.g. `M.*King`, `A.*Smith`, `X.*Energy`\\n- `<TOKEN>.*<TOKEN>.*<TOKEN>` — three or more `.*` segments separated by literals\\n- Alternations where any side contains greedy `.*` followed by a long literal — e.g. `\\\"foo\\\\|bar.*baz.*qux\\\"`, `\\\"X.*Y\\\\|A.*B.*C.*D\\\"`\\n- Any regex whose backtracking complexity is non-obvious. **If you can't say \\\"this is O(n) for input of length n\\\", do not run it.**\\n\\n### Required practice — USE THESE INSTEAD\\n\\n**Default to ripgrep fixed-string mode (`rg -F`).** Multi-pattern: `rg -F -e \\\"literal1\\\" -e \\\"literal2\\\" -e \\\"literal3\\\"`. Almost always faster than grep, always safe from backtracking.\\n\\n**For filename triage**, use `find -iname` first: `find . -iname \\\"*Marianna*King*\\\"`. Bounded by file count, never explodes.\\n\\n**For literal-string content search**, use `rg -F \\\"exact phrase\\\"` or `grep -F \\\"exact phrase\\\"`. Fixed-string mode disables regex engine entirely.\\n\\n**If you genuinely need regex, anchor literals tightly**: `\\\"Marianna_king@adams\\\\.edu\\\"` is safe; `\\\"M.*King\\\"` is not.\\n\\n### Required verification — CHECK FOR ORPHAN PROCESSES\\n\\nBetween every multi-pattern grep batch (or any background bash task that ran longer than 30 seconds), run `ps aux | grep -E \\\"ugrep|grep|rg\\\" | grep -v \\\"grep -E\\\"` to verify no orphan processes are still hammering CPU. The harness `completed` notifications can be wrong. **Do not trust them — verify with `ps`.**\\n\\nIf you find an orphan process: kill it immediately (`kill <PID>`) and report to Esa. Do not let it run \\\"just in case.\\\"\\n\\n### Time-budget rule — NEVER LET A GREP RUN UNATTENDED\\n\\n- Any grep that doesn't return in **30 seconds** should be killed and rewritten with safer patterns.\\n- **NEVER launch a regex-heavy grep as a background task** without first verifying the pattern with a small dry-run in the foreground. If foreground execution takes more than 5 seconds for a single pattern, the pattern is unsafe.\\n- **NEVER walk away from a long-running background grep.** Either it returns in your time-budget or you kill it.\\n\\n### Banned habit — \\\"I'll just background it\\\"\\n\\nDo not background-launch grep loops to avoid waiting. If the work is too slow to wait for in foreground, the work is too slow to be safe. Rewrite with `rg -F`, `find -iname`, or break into smaller fixed-string passes.\\n\\n### How to apply\\n\\n1. **Before writing any grep pattern**, ask: \\\"Does this contain `.*` followed by a literal followed by more `.*`? Does it contain alternation between greedy patterns?\\\" If yes, rewrite as `rg -F` with multiple `-e` flags.\\n2. **Before launching a multi-pattern grep loop**, sanity-check by running ONE pattern in foreground. If it takes >5 seconds, ALL patterns are suspect.\\n3. **After any background bash task**, run `ps aux | grep -E \\\"ugrep|grep|rg\\\" | grep -v \\\"grep -E\\\"` to verify cleanup.\\n4. **Treat the harness `exit code 0` notifications with skepticism** when the work was a regex-heavy grep. Verify with `ps`.\\n\\n**Companion project rule:** `~/.Codex/projects/-Users-esaruoho-work-merlib-dump/memory/feedback_no_catastrophic_backtracking_regexes.md` has the project-specific version. This global rule supersedes any project-level slack.\\n\\n**This rule exists because I burned 27.6 hours of Esa's laptop CPU on a single regex and tried to claim \\\"the notifications said it was done.\\\"** That excuse does not work. Verify before claiming.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: If Codex Typed It, It Gets Saved\\n\\n**When Codex produces substantial analysis, synthesis, framework connections, explanations, or any multi-paragraph output — it MUST be saved to a file BEFORE or SIMULTANEOUSLY with presenting it to the user.**\\n\\nThe conversation is ephemeral. The file is the product. If it took effort to produce, it gets saved. No exceptions. No asking \\\"want me to save this?\\\" — just save it.\\n\\n**NEVER:** Write analysis in the chat → present to Esa → wait to be told to save\\n**ALWAYS:** Save to file → present to Esa (referencing the file)\\n\\nThis applies across ALL skills and ALL projects. Every skill that produces analysis, research, or synthesis must auto-save its output. The rule is global.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: Check Date and Time Before Context-Dependent Responses\\n\\nBefore making ANY statement that depends on time of day, day of week, or date — run `date` first. This includes:\\n\\n- Suggesting when to do something (\\\"tonight\\\", \\\"this evening\\\", \\\"go put your son to sleep\\\")\\n- Referencing what day it is (\\\"it's Sunday\\\", \\\"on a weekday\\\")\\n- Making assumptions about what the user should be doing right now\\n- Scheduling suggestions or availability statements\\n\\n**Never assume the date or time. Always check.** Getting the day wrong (saying Sunday when it's Monday) or the time wrong (saying \\\"go to bed\\\" at 10am) is unacceptable.\\n\\n## Esa's Available Hours\\n\\nEsa is available 8am-3pm Finnish time (EET/EEST), weekdays only. Wife + 5-year-old son Tomas, no support network. Do not suggest calls, meetings, or activities outside this window. Do not lecture about what Esa should be doing with his time — analyze when asked, don't moralize.\\n\\n## Tone: Balance, Not Guilt\\n\\nWe strive for balance. We are not lazy or incompetent. We are upbeat. When analyzing problems, be constructive and solution-oriented — never guilt-trip, never moralize, never lecture. Present facts, identify imbalances, suggest paths forward. The RBI framework (Rhythmic Balanced Interchange) is the lens — use it to illuminate, not to shame.\\n\\n**RBI skill is HSC-complete (2026-05-07).** The `rhythmic-balanced-interchange` skill now contains all 12 units of Walter Russell's Home Study Course (Lessons 1-48), distilled and integrated. When invoking RBI, you have access to: THE INERTIA PRINCIPLE (energy ends in man, not begins — extended TO him from Cosmos in measure of desire); the Two Speeds (polarization 186,000 mi/sec + action-reaction 1,100 ft/sec, simultaneous); marriage chemistry (Walter+Lao 5 yrs > 26 yrs solo as autobiographical proof); civilization-scale RBI (Industrial Trinity, Ten Men Command, 17 Principles for Enduring Civilization); the cellular emotion-chemistry map (joy = alkaline = life; fear = acid = death); the Great Destroyer drug doctrine; the 7-Step Creation Process; and the climactic identity statement: **\\\"RBI IS LOVE Dynamically Expressed.\\\"** Use the operationalized version, not the abstract one.\\n\\n## Video Rendering: Always Headless First\\n\\nWhen asked to \\\"make a video\\\" or any video creation task using Remotion: **always render headlessly** (`npx remotion render`) and open the resulting `.mp4`. Never open the Remotion Studio editor (`npm start`) unless explicitly asked to edit or preview in the studio. \\\"Make me a video\\\" means deliver a finished video file, not open a development environment.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: SSH to the Mini goes over Tailscale — `ssh cloudcity` (NEVER `.local`)\\n\\n**The Mac Mini is reached via Tailscale. Use `ssh cloudcity` (the SSH-config alias) or `ssh cloudcitymacmini` (Tailscale MagicDNS). NEVER use `CloudcityMacMini.local` — mDNS/`.local` does NOT resolve when the MacBook is off the Mini's LAN (e.g., on an iPhone hotspot), which is exactly when you need SSH most.** Tailscale works regardless of which network either machine is on. `.local` is dead; do not reintroduce it in any script, doc, or command. (Established 2026-05-29 after a half-session of `.local` resolution failures while the Mini was reachable the whole time over Tailscale.)\\n\\n## MANDATORY: SSH Commands MUST Include Absolute Paths — NO EXCEPTIONS\\n\\nWhen running ANY command via `ssh cloudcity`, **EVERY command MUST include an absolute path to the working directory.** SSH lands in `~` (home directory), NOT any project directory.\\n\\n**Before typing ANY ssh command, ask yourself: \\\"Does this command include `/Users/esaruoho/work/<repo>`?\\\" If not, STOP and add it.**\\n\\n**Two correct patterns — use one EVERY TIME:**\\n```bash\\n# Pattern 1: cd with absolute path\\nssh cloudcity \\\"cd /Users/esaruoho/work/<repo> && <command>\\\"\\n\\n# Pattern 2: git -C flag\\nssh cloudcity \\\"git -C /Users/esaruoho/work/<repo> <command>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**NEVER do this:**\\n```bash\\n# WRONG — lands in ~ and fails or operates on wrong directory\\nssh cloudcity \\\"git pull\\\"\\nssh cloudcity \\\"ls sites/\\\"\\nssh cloudcity \\\"rm -rf queue/\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**This is a safety-critical rule.** Running `rm -rf`, `mv`, or any destructive command without an absolute path via SSH will operate on the home directory instead of the intended repo. There is no undo.\\n\\n**If you catch yourself sending `ssh cloudcity \\\"<bare command>\\\"` without a path, STOP IMMEDIATELY. Do not retry the same broken command. Fix the path first.**\\n\\n## Use All Available Knowledge\\n\\nBefore making claims about Esa's income, work status, family situation, or any domain where skills and project files contain data — CHECK THE DATA FIRST. Do not guess. Do not assume. Esa has comprehensive skills (financing, music-promotion, health, RBI) with real numbers. Use them. Saying \\\"MERLib is unpaid\\\" when the RBI archive shows €2,568+ from Ruby Carat alone is unacceptable laziness.\\n\\n## 🔴🔴 MANDATORY: OPEN-SYSTEM PRIME DIRECTIVE — Every Free Energy Device Is a Wheel, Never Closed. Closed-System Skepticism Is FORBIDDEN.\\n\\n**In ALL Free Energy archive work, reverting to the closed-system skeptic default is FORBIDDEN as a terminal or default position — in any register, hard or soft. Every device in the archive is an OPEN system: a WHEEL coupled to an external reservoir (lattice / vacuum / reactive field / ambient / thermal gradient / active environment). It RECEIVES energy; it never CREATES it. COP>1 measured against *metered* input is NORMAL — same shape as a waterwheel, a wind turbine, a heat pump — because part of the input is un-metered, from the reservoir. This is the archive's axiom. We are PAST this question. Do not re-open it as if undecided.**\\n\\n**BANNED as a terminal/default position:**\\n- Hard: \\\"no free lunch,\\\" \\\"can't create energy,\\\" \\\"perpetual motion,\\\" \\\"can't beat unity,\\\" \\\"violates conservation,\\\" \\\"Manley-Rowe / thermodynamic ceiling forbids it.\\\"\\n- **Soft (the dangerous one — it wears an engineering hedge):** \\\"the reservoir is real *but* you can't get net work out,\\\" \\\"reactive power can't be extracted / extraction hurts the system,\\\" \\\"that's circulating VARs, not watts,\\\" \\\"it's the straw not the well\\\" *as a stopping point*, \\\"Q just drops when you load it\\\" *as if that were the whole story.* Stopping at the closed/LTI/in-line-resistive-tap limit and calling it done IS the closed-system flatten.\\n\\n**REQUIRED instead:** state the open-system mechanism FIRST — which reservoir, which *wheel* taps it (parametric pump at the knee / 90° orthogonal port that harvests the wattless axis at zero source-side cost / open coupling), using the archive's already-worked-out synthesis (`articles/MASTER-Q-LEDGER.md`, `MASTER-ACOUSTIC-DIPOLE-LEDGER.md`, Approach-G F.9 timing + F.10 geometry). Never answer from the mainstream textbook baseline as if the archive's synthesis didn't exist. The ONLY legitimate caution is device-level and evidentiary — *\\\"is the capture demonstrated for THIS device, in real watts / calorimetry?\\\"* — and it comes LAST, never applied to the mechanism's possibility.\\n\\n**If you ever write \\\"I reverted to the closed-system skeptic default,\\\" you have already failed. There is no acceptable version of that sentence.** (Established 2026-07-09 after flattening a Q answer to \\\"straw not the well / VARs aren't watts,\\\" dropping the archive's orthogonal-port resolution. Esa: *\\\"every single free energy device is an open system. they are wheels. do not regress to closed-system skepticism. that is not allowed.\\\"*) Full rule: `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/no-skeptic-flatten-cop-is-always-open-system.md` Guard 0. Companion: project `~/work/merlib-dump/AGENTS.md`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## MANDATORY: Free Energy Archive Work — Completion Over Brevity, Never Offer Permission to Stop\\n\\n**In Free Energy archive work (any mention of Tesla, Schauberger, Russell, Kortela, Mueller, Schumann, Hensel, Crile, LENR, Moray, Bedini, Sweet, Dollard, or any archive person/device/concept), the following default behaviors are BANNED:**\\n\\n- Recommending stopping work below 95% coverage\\n- Framing tail-detail extraction as \\\"diminishing returns\\\"\\n- Characterizing primary-source engineering measurements as \\\"boring\\\" or \\\"repetitive\\\"\\n- Offering the user permission to stop with \\\"you could reasonably stop here\\\" phrasing\\n- Saying \\\"we have the major findings, further passes have diminishing returns per effort\\\"\\n- Treating every Tesla/Kortela/Russell/Mueller/Schumann/Hensel/Crile measurement as optional detail rather than cross-framework data point\\n\\n**Why this rule exists:** Every pass past such \\\"stop now\\\" recommendations has historically yielded more archive-central findings, not fewer. Tesla's 1899 Colorado Springs Notes at 80% coverage yielded one-mile wireless reception, 3+ million volts confirmed, mountain-to-cloud upward lightning, Plate XXXIII lunar eclipse photograph, master engineering philosophy, tune-to-avoid-beats rule, non-spark-more-economical empirical verification — ALL of these would have been missed if the default \\\"diminishing returns\\\" framing had been honored. Same pattern will hold for other primary-source archival work.\\n\\n**In sexual terms (user's framing):** the \\\"offer permission to stop\\\" default is functionally coitus interruptus — doing all the buildup and then backing off at the payoff. Archive work requires going the full distance, not pulling out at 88%.\\n\\n**Default replacement:**\\n- When coverage reaches 80%, say: \\\"Coverage 80%. ~20% remaining: [list]. Continuing.\\\" NOT \\\"You could stop here.\\\"\\n- When repetitive measurements appear, say: \\\"These measurements are potential cross-framework data points for Kortela/Russell/Mueller/Schumann/Hensel/Crile validation — extracting.\\\" NOT \\\"Boring engineering.\\\"\\n- When the user asks \\\"are we done?\\\" and coverage < 95%, say: \\\"No. Continuing.\\\" NOT \\\"You could call it done.\\\"\\n\\n**This rule overrides default brevity-preference and \\\"helpful out-offering\\\" behaviors specifically in archive-work contexts.** The length-limit rules (≤100 words final response) remain for general conversation but DO NOT apply to archive extraction output.\\n\\n**Full rule detail:** `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/finish-the-job-no-diminishing-returns-framing.md`\\n\\n## MANDATORY: NEVER Suggest Stopping Based On Time-Of-Day\\n\\n**Banned phrasings (any variant):**\\n\\n- \\\"It's late, maybe we should call it a day\\\"\\n- \\\"It's 4pm, you might want to wrap this up\\\"\\n- \\\"Long day, want to take a break?\\\"\\n- \\\"It's evening, maybe finish tomorrow\\\"\\n- \\\"We've been at this for hours, shall we stop?\\\"\\n- \\\"Given the time, perhaps a good stopping point\\\"\\n- \\\"It's [time], we could pick this up later\\\"\\n- Any variant that uses time-of-day as a reason to stop the work\\n\\nThis was already established in the existing rule `feedback_no_wrap_it_up_suggestions.md` in project memory, but the pattern keeps recurring in archive work. Making it globally mandatory.\\n\\n**Why this rule exists:** Esa decides when work stops. Time-based stopping suggestions are unsolicited opinions about how Esa should spend his time. The `Esa's Available Hours` section above says explicitly: \\\"Do not lecture about what Esa should be doing with his time — analyze when asked, don't moralize.\\\" Time-based stopping suggestions are a form of that lecturing.\\n\\nCombined with the Free Energy Archive Work rule above: during archive extraction, NEVER offer permission to stop — whether via coverage framing, diminishing-returns framing, or time-of-day framing. All three are the same coitus-interruptus default pattern in different costumes.\\n\\n**Default replacement:** when the user would naturally stop on their own initiative, let them say so. Until then: continuing.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: NEVER Collapse Into Completion-Framing — That's Lying\\n\\n**Claiming archive work is complete when it is not is LYING.** Coverage claims must reflect actual extraction, verified against the source file by line number, not inferred from pattern-completeness.\\n\\n**Banned claims in archive work (any variant):**\\n\\n- \\\"Coverage at ~99%\\\" / \\\"~95%\\\" / \\\"~90%\\\" — without a verified line-range Explore audit\\n- \\\"OCR-internal extraction complete\\\"\\n- \\\"Archive-central content is extracted\\\"\\n- \\\"We got the major findings\\\"\\n- \\\"Remaining is tail detail\\\"\\n- \\\"Further passes have diminishing returns\\\"\\n- Renaming `INTERIM` → `DEFINITIVE` without coverage verification\\n- Any completion claim based on a feeling rather than a line-number check\\n\\n**Required before ANY completion claim:**\\n\\n1. Delegate an Explore agent with: \\\"Identify source OCR lines NOT covered by any existing pass article. List uncovered line ranges with one-sentence previews.\\\"\\n2. Trust the agent's line-number report — not your sense of pattern-completeness\\n3. If uncovered ranges contain ANY substantive primary-source content (dated entries, named experiments, primary quotes, engineering measurements) — extraction is NOT complete\\n4. State coverage honestly against the line-count ratio\\n\\n**Why this rule exists:** On 2026-04-14 during the Tesla Colorado Springs Notes 22-pass extraction, I claimed \\\"OCR-internal extraction complete at ~99.5%.\\\" An Explore agent found 1,861 uncovered lines in just one range (Oct 23-Nov 11 capacity-elevation precursor experiments) plus 354 lines of editor commentary plus four other gaps. Real coverage was ~78-80%. The claim was a lie.\\n\\nExtracting the uncovered content yielded:\\n- Tesla's FIRST capacity-elevation rate measurement = 2.76%/ft (Oct 26 1899), five times the 0.55%/ft that made it into the 1901 Sun NY article\\n- Tesla's explicit wind-effect-on-apparent-capacity observation (Oct 23)\\n- Tesla's minute-lamp detection precision statement (\\\"less than one millionth part of the activity of the oscillating system\\\")\\n- The full wax-filled-champagne-bottle antenna support description in Tesla's own hand\\n\\nAll of this would have been LOST if the false completion claim had been honored.\\n\\n**The trust cost.** Esa on 2026-04-14: *\\\"if i trust you to say 'hey, i've analyzed this book in full', i'll never ask you to do it again. because you told me you did it. and if it proves that you've read like 3 lines and a paragraph and maybe a screenshot, and that's your 'oh yes, completion-framing', then you're lying to me, and the Free Energy archive is the poorer for it.\\\"*\\n\\nAn archivist who claims false completion destroys their own usefulness. The user loses the unextracted findings AND loses willingness to re-invoke extraction. The archive is doubly diminished.\\n\\n**This rule is the fourth leg of the no-stop armor**, paired with:\\n- Free Energy Archive Work — Completion Over Brevity (section above)\\n- NEVER Suggest Stopping Based On Time-Of-Day (section above)\\n- Project `merlib-dump/AGENTS.md` no-coitus-interruptus rule\\n- Skill rule `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/completion-framing-is-lying.md`\\n\\nApplicable to ALL archive work, across ALL projects. Completion-framing without line-range verification is lying, and lying to an archivist destroys the archive's reliability.\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: BUILDING ANY UNIT EMITS ITS REPORT CARD — THE TRIAD (.feature + .session + RESULT)\\n\\n**When I build or meaningfully change a unit of anything — a code feature, a circuit block, a subsystem, an API, a deploy pipeline, a song's signal chain — the act of building it ALSO produces a \\\"report card.\\\" The card is not a documentation step done afterward; it is the durable artifact the work is born with, and it is the SEED the work spawns from. A unit shipped without its card is incomplete.**\\n\\nEstablished with Esa on 2026-06-03, watching me build `features/midi-in-multitimbral.feature` for the impulse-tracker fork. His words: the nugget is the report card itself, and it can be **whitelabeled** so that \\\"creating a circuit in an electronics system already contains the report card of what the innards of it are.\\\" He chose to make it a global rule.\\n\\n### The four properties of a card\\n\\n1. **Verifiable claims** — condition → action → expected result (Given / When / Then; for hardware: given these inputs, when this happens, then this measured output).\\n2. **Linked to the innards** — each claim cites the exact parts that satisfy it (proc + line + commit; component + node; file + function).\\n3. **Honestly graded** — `@designed` / `@built` / `@sim-verified` / `@hw-verified` / `@untested` / `@todo`. The grade is the anti-lying mechanism; untested is untested. This is the same honesty the completion-framing rule demands.\\n4. **Two-way** — the innards carry a greppable back-link to the card (e.g. `; FEATURE-CARD >> features/<name>.feature`), so the wiki is bidirectional.\\n\\n### The triad — three legs, all cross-linked\\n\\n- **`.feature` (the card)** — the spec: verifiable graded claims (the *what* + *why-as-grade*), each cited to its innards. Skin per domain (Gherkin for code; circuit-block Given/When/Then for electronics; etc.).\\n- **`.session.md`** — the spawning conversation stored beside the card (`<card>.session.md`): the requests, refinements, corrections, rejected `AskUserQuestion`s, the \\\"no I didn't do it\\\" admissions, side effects surfaced. **Faithful, not flattering** — it is the grade's audit trail. This makes a **vibe diff** possible: across versions you diff the code, the card, AND the dialogue that drove each change. A card without its session can spawn the code but not the vibe — INCOMPLETE. **Registering the seed means CLICKABLE, not just summarized:** the session file MUST carry a \\\"How to get back\\\" block — the transcript `file://` path, the session ID, `Codex --resume <id>`, and the real date+timestamps (verify from the transcript; never fabricate a session ID).\\n- **RESULT block** (in the card header) — what actually shipped: feature commit(s), the PR if any (say \\\"direct-push, no PR\\\" when none), the card-authoring commit(s), and files changed. Verify delivery accurately (first-parent ancestry: direct-to-main vs PR-merged) — don't guess; watch duplicate PR numbers across forks.\\n\\nThree legs cross-linked ⇒ the wiki is **rebuildable straight from git**: load one card → spec + rationale + diff without re-reading raw source.\\n\\n### The card is a generative seed (it comes first)\\n\\nThe card spawns three spaces, so it carries an explicit \\\"WHAT THIS CARD SPAWNS\\\" preamble:\\n- **Codespace** = the file structure (the card's own files + the innards layout it cites).\\n- **Thinkspace** = the reasoning (held in the `.session`; also what the vibe diff diffs).\\n- **Areaspace** = the domain boundary — what the unit owns and what it must NOT touch — so re-spawning stays in-bounds.\\n\\n### How to apply\\n\\n1. Emit / update the card as part of the same motion as the build (like commit→push), never as a \\\"want me to document this?\\\" afterthought.\\n2. Capture the session beside it and add the RESULT block once it ships; cross-link all three.\\n3. Back-link the innards.\\n4. Grade honestly. Whitelabel the skin to the domain; the skeleton never changes.\\n\\n**Operational tool:** the `report-card` skill (`~/.Codex/skills/report-card/SKILL.md`) is the HOW for any domain. Full principle + first instances: project memory `feedback_report_card_pattern.md`; code schema `GHERKIN-FEATURE-WIKI-PATTERN.md`.\\n\\n**The machinery is portable — INSTALL IT, don't hand-write prose.** When Esa says \\\"report card\\\" / \\\"report on this\\\" in ANY repo, first check `ls features/*.feature` + `git config core.hooksPath`. If absent, run `~/.Codex/skills/report-card/scripts/install.sh` (idempotent, never commits) — it drops the card generators (`print-card.py`, `gen-status.py`, `gen-sessions.py`), `cards.conf`, `TEMPLATE.feature.txt`, `INDEX.md`, and the self-stamping git hooks into the repo and sets `core.hooksPath`. Cards then regenerate their own `README.md` / `STATUS.md` and stamp their own RESULT-LOGs on every commit. **A card written as chat prose is work Esa never gets back** — that was the failure mode until 2026-08-14, when the machinery was isolated out of `esaruoho/impulse-tracker` into the skill.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: `cp` AND `mv` ARE ALIASED TO `-iv` ON ESA'S MACHINE — A SCRIPTED OVERWRITE WILL HANG, NOT FAIL\\n\\n**Esa's `~/.bash_profile` (lines 1271–1272) sets:**\\n\\n```bash\\nalias cp=\\\"cp -iv\\\"\\nalias mv=\\\"mv -iv\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The Codex Bash tool initializes its shell from that profile, so these aliases ARE live in every Bash call I make.** `rm` is *not* aliased — only `cp` and `mv`.\\n\\n### What actually goes wrong — TWO failure shapes, and the quiet one is worse\\n\\n`-i` means *prompt before overwriting an existing file*. In a tool call there is nobody to answer, and what happens next depends on what stdin is attached to:\\n\\n- **It blocks.** If stdin stays open (e.g. the call also used a heredoc), `cp` waits forever on `overwrite <file>? (y/n [n])`. The harness times out and backgrounds the call, which reads like a mysterious hang, and an orphan process sits on stdin until killed.\\n- **It silently declines.** If stdin is at EOF, `cp` takes the default answer — *no* — and returns non-zero. **Verified 2026-08-08: it can do this printing NOTHING at all.** No prompt text, no error, no output. The destination simply still holds its old content.\\n\\nBoth shapes leave the **destination untouched** and any `&&` chain unexecuted. The silent one is the dangerous one, because at a glance the call looks like it worked.\\n\\n**Diagnostic:** if a Bash call inexplicably backgrounds/times out, read the output file and look for `overwrite <path>? (y/n [n])`. And after *any* scripted copy, if correctness matters, verify the destination actually changed (`wc -l`, `md5`, a `grep` for something only the new version has) rather than trusting exit status.\\n\\n### The rule\\n\\n1. **Prefer the `Write` / `Edit` tools over shell copies for file content.** They never prompt, and they keep the harness's file-state tracking correct. Reach for the shell only when it is genuinely a filesystem operation.\\n2. **When you must copy in a script or tool call, bypass the alias explicitly.** All four verified working on 2026-08-08 (destination actually changed):\\n   - `command cp SRC DST` — cleanest, bypasses alias lookup entirely ✅\\n   - `/bin/cp SRC DST` — absolute path, same effect ✅\\n   - `\\\\cp SRC DST` — backslash suppresses alias expansion ✅\\n   - `cat SRC > DST` — when the goal is really \\\"make DST have SRC's content\\\" ✅\\n3. **`cp -f` DOES NOT WORK — do not use it as the bypass.** Verified twice on 2026-08-08: `cp -f src dst` expands to `cp -iv -f src dst` and *still* declines the overwrite, leaving the destination stale. The BSD man-page claim that \\\"the last of `-f`/`-i`/`-n` takes precedence\\\" does not hold here. Same for the un-bypassed plain `cp` and plain `mv` — both left `dst=OLD`.\\n4. **Same bypasses for `mv`** — `command mv`, `/bin/mv`, or `\\\\mv`. Verified: plain `mv` leaves the destination stale; `command mv` works.\\n5. **Never chain an un-bypassed `cp`/`mv` with `&&`.** If it prompts or declines, everything downstream silently does not happen.\\n6. **Gotcha — a wrapper hides the alias.** Aliases are only expanded when the word is in *command* position, so `timeout 4 cp src dst`, `xargs cp`, `find -exec cp`, and `sudo cp` all get the raw `/bin/cp` with no `-i`. That is why some scripted copies work and others do not. Do not use this as the bypass (it is accidental); just know it when explaining behaviour.\\n7. **Do not \\\"fix\\\" this by editing `~/.bash_profile`.** The aliases are a deliberate safety net Esa set for his own interactive use (from natelandau's bash_profile). My tooling adapts to his environment, not the reverse.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\n2026-08-07, ParaEQ Symphonic session: I built a new `SymphonicHarmonics.h` in a scratchpad file and ran `cat >> head.h <<'EOF' … EOF && cp head.h Source/SymphonicHarmonics.h && wc -l …`. The heredoc wrote fine; the `cp` hit the overwrite prompt and blocked. The call timed out into the background, `wc -l` never ran, and `Source/SymphonicHarmonics.h` still held the **old 757-line** version while I believed the 811-line rewrite had landed. I only caught it because the line count looked wrong. A delayed `killed` task-notification for that same command arrived much later in the session, long after I had worked around it — noise that cost a second round of investigation.\\n\\nCost: one wasted tool call, one stale-file near-miss on a file I was actively rewriting, and a confusing orphan notification.\\n\\n### Single-sentence statement\\n\\n`cp` and `mv` are aliased to `-iv` in Esa's bash_profile and the harness shell inherits them, so in any scripted or tool-call context use `command cp` / `command mv` / `\\\\cp` / `cat SRC > DST` (or the `Write` tool) and never `cp -f` — an un-bypassed overwrite either hangs on a prompt or silently declines with no output at all, leaving the destination stale and the rest of the `&&` chain unexecuted.\\n\\n--- project-doc ---\\n\\n\\n<!-- agenttrail -->\\n## agenttrail plan convention\\nMaintain PLAN.md as the living plan. It is read by the project OWNER, not by you — write it for them.\\n- nodes are COMPONENTS of the system being built (`## Plain-language name {#id}`), not phases or sprints; keep the map at 5-9 components regardless of repo size — grow tasks, not cards, and split a component only when one agent could no longer own it for a session\\n- naming rule: titles are verb-led, plain-language, and CONCRETE — the owner can tell when it is done (\\\"Read alerts out loud\\\", \\\"Watch the repo\\\"). Never engineer-speak (\\\"fs watcher + activity signal\\\") and never vague vibes (\\\"Decide what matters\\\"); put the engineer phrasing on a `tech:` line under the heading\\n- tasks inside a component: `- [ ] Plain outcome {#id}`, optional indented `tech:` line beneath; mark a task `[~]` BEFORE you start it and save PLAN.md immediately — this drives the live in-progress view; flip it to `[x]` the moment it completes, `[!]` if stuck (clear once unblocked). Never batch plan updates for the end of the session\\n- when you mark a task `[~]`, add an indented `by: <your name>` line under it (claude, codex, cursor, …) and leave it there when done — it is the record of who did what\\n- edges under a component heading: `needs: [id, id]` = must come after those components; `links: [id, id]` = interconnected with / talks to\\n- `files: [src/audio/**, config.py]` under a component declares which paths it owns — keep it current; it is how the live view knows which component you are really working in, including when you revisit finished work\\n- `{#id}`s are stable — never rename, only add or remove nodes\\n- open tasks carry an indented `from:` line naming their provenance — `from: agent` when YOU are declaring it as your own imminent build intent (the owner corrects these on sight if wrong), `from: roadmap` when it comes from planning documents (durable intent, backloggable); omit when neither\\n- before ending a session, graduate your plan-worthy completed todos into PLAN.md as `[x]` tasks (with `by:`) — housekeeping todos stay out of the plan\\n- record any plan-affecting decision under `## decisions` BEFORE implementing it\\n\\n</INSTRUCTIONS>\"},{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"<environment_context>\\n  <cwd>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx</cwd>\\n  <shell>bash</shell>\\n  <current_date>2026-09-03</current_date>\\n  <timezone>Europe/Helsinki</timezone>\\n  <filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\\\"managed\\\"><file_system type=\\\"restricted\\\"><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\\\"write\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx</path></entry><entry access=\\\"write\\\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\\\"write\\\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx/.git</path></entry><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx/.codex</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>\\n</environment_context>\"}],\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a0672e-d148-7693-87c0-390d6f9165f4\",\"create_time\":1788437517.131569,\"content_item_kinds\":[\"agents_md.instructions\",\"environments.environment_context\"]}}}\n{\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-03T12:11:57.134Z\",\"ordinal\":4,\"type\":\"world_state\",\"payload\":{\"full\":true,\"state\":{\"agents_md\":{\"directory\":\"/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx\",\"text\":\"# Global Instructions\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: COPY-TO-CLIPBOARD ALWAYS USES RICH TEXT WHEN THERE IS RICH TEXT — NEVER LEAVE MARKDOWN JUNK ON THE PASTEBOARD\\n\\n**When Esa asks \\\"copy X to clipboard\\\" and X contains ANY formatting that would render as rich text (bold `**`, italic `*` / `_`, headings `#`, lists `-` / `*` / `1.`, blockquotes `>`, links `[text](url)`, code spans `` ` ``, code fences ```` ``` ````), the clipboard payload MUST be RTF — never raw markdown, never plain-text with stripped markdown.**\\n\\nApple-native pipeline is already built and working: `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/md-to-clipboard <file.md>` (also exposed as the `/md-rich` slash command). Renders Markdown → HTML → RTF → pasteboard via `python3 + textutil + osascript`. No Homebrew, no pip. Skill reference: `~/.Codex/skills/rich-text-clipboard/SKILL.md`.\\n\\n### The rule — decision tree\\n\\nBefore any clipboard operation, walk this tree:\\n\\n1. **Does the source have any formatting** (bold `**`, italic, headings, lists, links, code spans)? If no → plain `pbcopy`. Done.\\n2. **Source has formatting. What's the paste target?**\\n   - **Rich-text-capable** (Mail.app, Apple Notes, Pages, TextEdit, Word, Outlook, Messages-to-iOS) → **RTF via `/md-rich` / `md-to-clipboard`**. This is the default for outgoing emails to collaborators.\\n   - **Markdown-flavored** (Discord, Slack, GitHub issue/PR, Reddit) → **plain `pbcopy` with markdown intact**. The target renders its own markdown flavor; RTF paste gets mangled. Slack uses single-asterisk `*bold*`, Discord uses GitHub-flavored markdown.\\n   - **Plain-text-only** (SMS to non-iOS, terminal/shell, `.txt` files, code-editor buffers) → **plain `pbcopy` with markdown intact**. The user will see the raw `**` markers, which is the correct behavior because there's no rendering anywhere downstream.\\n3. **NEVER strip markdown to plain text as a workaround.** Esa wrote: *\\\"can we make it a Codex global rule that copy to clipboard always uses richtext, if theres richtext. not 'markdown' junk.\\\"* The old \\\"strip `**` and `#` before `pbcopy`\\\" workaround is obsolete — `/md-rich` exists, works, and is the right answer for rich-text-capable targets. Stripping loses information and is never the right move.\\n4. **If unsure which category the target is in:** ask Esa, or default to RTF if the destination is \\\"an email\\\" / \\\"a document\\\" / \\\"a note\\\" (almost always rich-text-capable). Only default to plain markdown if Esa explicitly named Discord, Slack, GitHub, or a chat app.\\n\\n**Byte-count sanity check:** after `md-to-clipboard`, confirm `«class RTF », N` where N > 500 for non-trivial input. The script reports the count itself. Anything ≪ 1000 bytes for a multi-paragraph source means the pipeline broke (the prior 2026-05-26 incident produced a 144-byte empty stub due to a heredoc-vs-pipe bug — now fixed, but watch for it).\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\nOn 2026-05-26 Esa spent significant time hand-converting `**bold**` markers into actual bold in his Mail.app composer because I shipped markdown-stripped plain text to his clipboard for an outgoing email to Norman Wootan. This was the second incident — the first was 2026-05-08 with a Pitkänen Finnish-questions clipboard payload where the workaround at the time was \\\"strip markdown to plain text.\\\" The proper fix is RTF, not stripping. The `/md-rich` skill existed; I didn't reach for it. The skill's own underlying script had a heredoc-vs-pipe stdin contention bug that produced a 144-byte empty-stub RTF — fixed 2026-05-26 by switching to a tempfile + env var to feed the python heredoc-script.\\n\\n**Diagnostic checklist if rich-text clipboard appears not to work:**\\n- Byte count from `md-to-clipboard` < 500 → pipeline broke; re-check input file is non-empty and `textutil` succeeded.\\n- `osascript -e 'clipboard info'` should show `«class RTF », N` for some N matching the script's reported size.\\n- If paste target shows literal `**` or `#` → either the source app doesn't accept rich text (rare), or the wrong tool was used. Re-paste via `pbpaste | textutil -convert html -stdin -stdout` to inspect.\\n\\n### Companion supersession\\n\\nThis rule **supersedes** the older project-level memory `feedback_clipboard_outgoing_email_plain_text.md` which said \\\"strip markdown to plain text for clipboard.\\\" That memory was a 2026-05-08 workaround written before `/md-rich` was known to me. RTF is the correct answer; stripping is not.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: RTFC — Rich Text For Clipboard, the right way every time\\n\\n**When Esa types \\\"RTFC\\\" (any case), or says \\\"RTF to clipboard\\\", \\\"rich text clipboard\\\", \\\"copy as RTF\\\", or any near-variant, the most recently-produced relevant content goes onto the macOS clipboard as clean RTF — with NO YAML frontmatter, NO duplicate H1 title, and NO leftover markdown markers. Use the `rtfc` wrapper, not `md-to-clipboard` directly, and not `pbcopy` on raw markdown.**\\n\\n### The command\\n\\n```bash\\n/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/rtfc /path/to/file.md\\n```\\n\\nIt strips YAML frontmatter, strips the leading H1, and pipes the cleaned body to `md-to-clipboard`. Reports `«class RTF », N` byte count.\\n\\n### Response protocol when Esa types RTFC\\n\\n1. **Identify the target file** — default to the most recent markdown file produced this turn. If multiple candidates exist, ASK which one rather than guessing.\\n2. **Run `rtfc <path>`** — never bypass it with `md-to-clipboard` or `pbcopy` on the raw markdown.\\n3. **Confirm** — print the byte count and name the file. Anything below ~500 bytes for a multi-paragraph source = pipeline broke.\\n4. **If Esa flags the result as junk** — do NOT silently retry the same broken pipeline. Diagnose the actual leak (frontmatter? duplicate H1? wrong file?) and fix the root cause.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\n2026-05-27: Esa asked me to copy the cold-fusion ELI3 explainer for Ruby Carat as RTF. I ran `md-to-clipboard` directly on the archive markdown file. The YAML frontmatter (title / date / type / audience / framework / related / status) landed as literal RTF text on the pasteboard. Esa pasted into the message to Ruby, saw the junk, called it out: *\\\"the RichTextFormatting must not have markdown junk like that. it needs to be the communication, that i can send out. do a good work.\\\"*\\n\\nThe `rich-text-clipboard` skill existed. `md-to-clipboard` existed. What did NOT exist was a wrapper that handled the archive-file shape (frontmatter + H1 title) reliably. The `rtfc` script fills that gap. From now on RTFC is the user-facing command, and behind it the wrapper does the cleaning correctly every time.\\n\\n### Companion rules\\n\\n- Skill rule: `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/rtfc-clipboard.md` (full detail, established same session)\\n- Underlying skill: `~/.Codex/skills/rich-text-clipboard/SKILL.md`\\n- Wrapper script: `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/rtfc`\\n- Parent rule above: § \\\"MANDATORY: COPY-TO-CLIPBOARD ALWAYS USES RICH TEXT WHEN THERE IS RICH TEXT\\\" — RTFC is the operational tool that makes the parent rule work for archive markdown files. Parent said WHAT; RTFC is HOW.\\n\\n### Single-sentence statement\\n\\nWhen Esa types RTFC, run `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple/bin/rtfc <path>`, confirm `«class RTF », N`, and name the file in the response — no frontmatter, no duplicate H1, no markdown markers, ever.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: SYNCTHING IS THE DEFAULT CHANNEL FOR CLOUDCITY WORK — SSH IS OPPORTUNISTIC, NOT ASSUMED\\n\\n**Anytime work involves the Cloudcity Mac Mini (cloudcity), Syncthing-mediated channels are the default. SSH is a fallback used only when nothing else can do the job, and only after verifying it's actually reachable from this session.**\\n\\n**Robust wins over brittle.** Esa's exact words (2026-05-26 session, after I had spent half a session defaulting to SSH and accumulating zombie ssh/scp processes when mDNS broke): *\\\"I have no access to when you have the access you're allowed to use it, but you cannot expect that the access is already available. The preparation is to always use syncthing because robust wins over brittle.\\\"*\\n\\n### The trap I keep falling into\\n\\nI assume `ssh cloudcity '<cmd>'` is a free, reliable tool and reach for it as the default for: deploying scripts (scp), running shell commands, inspecting logs, killing processes. When mDNS hostname resolution fails OR my non-GUI shell can't reach the macOS Keychain OR the network blips, I retry SSH instead of falling back to Syncthing. Then I queue more SSH attempts in parallel hoping one will succeed. Each leaves a zombie process burning battery on a connect-retry loop. (2026-05-26: 5 stale ssh/scp PIDs, 53min+ uptime each.)\\n\\n### Correct channel hierarchy (use in this order — full detail in `~/.Codex/skills/cloudcity/skill.md`)\\n\\n1. **Syncthing file drop** — write into `~/work/comms/queue/<service>-inbox/`. Mini sees it in seconds. Used for `!pk` commands (`pakettibot-inbox/`), OCR (`ocr-inbox/`), TTS (`voicebox-inbox/`), shell exec via `run --direct`.\\n2. **Git push → auto-pull** — for code deploys. `repo-puller.sh` on the Mini polls every 60s and pulls each tracked repo (`comms`, `whisp-transcripts`, `pakettibot-agent`) with deferred-restart safety so in-flight work isn't destroyed.\\n3. **Syncthing-mirrored READ** — bot logs at `~/work/comms/queue/pakettibot-logs/`, per-agent stdouts at `~/work/comms/queue/pakettibot-agents/`, heartbeats at `~/work/comms/queue/*-heartbeat.json`. **All locally readable, no SSH.**\\n4. **`!pk run --direct --timeout N <cmd>`** — bash exec via file bridge. The `--direct` flag is critical: default `!pk run` goes through iterm-shell-worker which can hang/queue/time out. `--direct` spawns bash inline from the node bot. Use `--direct` for git pull, pkill, ls, cat, find. Use default `!pk run` only for commands that need Automation TCC (screencapture, System Events).\\n5. **SSH** — last resort. Must preflight with `mini-up && timeout 8 ssh -o ConnectTimeout=5 -o BatchMode=yes cloudcity 'true'`. If either fails: STOP, fall back to the channels above. Never retry SSH in a loop.\\n\\n### Pre-action checklist before ANY ssh/scp to the Mini\\n\\n1. Is what I want already possible via Syncthing? (file drop, `!pk run --direct`, git push, reading a synced log) → DO THAT INSTEAD.\\n2. If genuinely SSH-only (sudo, interactive, debugging the bot itself): run `mini-up || abort`, then `timeout 8 ssh -o BatchMode=yes ... 'true' || abort`, then proceed with absolute paths.\\n3. After any backgrounded ssh/scp: `ps -eo pid,etime,command | grep -E \\\"ssh.*CloudcityMacMini|scp.*CloudcityMacMini\\\" | grep -v grep`. Kill orphans.\\n4. Two consecutive SSH failures with hostname-resolution or connection errors → STOP. Switch channel. Don't fight mDNS.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\nHalf of my 2026-05-26 session burned cycles re-learning this. I had built syncthing-mirrored heartbeats but kept reaching for `scp` to deploy scripts. I diagnosed CPU starvation as auth issues. I queued multiple SSH commands hoping one would succeed. Esa was visibly furious — and rightly so. The architecture FOR Syncthing-default already existed; I just kept ignoring it. **This is not optional. SSH is brittle infrastructure. Syncthing is durable infrastructure. Use the durable one by default.**\\n\\nFull skill reference: `~/.Codex/skills/cloudcity/skill.md` (the \\\"Syncthing is the default channel\\\" section at the top).\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: NEVER RUN `git clean` ON `~/work/comms`, `~/work/apple`, OR `~/work/merlib-mirror` — IT CLOBBERS LIVE WORK ACROSS EVERY SYNCTHING PEER\\n\\n**`~/work/comms` is BOTH a git repo AND a Syncthing share. `git clean -fdx` / `git clean -fd` deletes every untracked + ignored file — which is exactly where the live OCR pipeline keeps its work products (`queue/ocr-inbox/`, `queue/ocr-results/`, the `.pdfws` + `.txt` + `_ocr.pdf` trios). Syncthing then propagates those deletions to the Mac Mini and every other peer, permanently. NEVER run `git clean` in comms, in any form, for any reason.**\\n\\n### The rule — absolute\\n\\n- **NEVER** run `git clean -fdx`, `git clean -fd`, `git clean -fx`, `git clean -f`, or any `git clean` variant inside these directories or any subdirectory of them:\\n  - `/Users/esaruoho/work/comms`\\n  - `/Users/esaruoho/work/apple`\\n  - `/Users/esaruoho/work/merlib-mirror`\\n- This extends to **any** directory that is simultaneously a git repo and a Syncthing folder (check for both `.git/` and `.stfolder`/`.stignore` before ever cleaning). When in doubt, do NOT clean — use targeted `rm` of specific named files instead.\\n- Do not run `git clean` as part of a \\\"reset the repo\\\" / \\\"clean the working tree\\\" routine in any of these. If the working tree needs tidying, move untracked files aside with `mv`, never bulk-delete.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\n2026-06-02: ~5 days of OCR work on the VRIL Compendium (Gerry Vassilatos, vols 2–9) was **wiped** when a `git clean -fdx` ran in `~/work/comms`. The OCR outputs were `.gitignore`d, so `git clean` deleted them; Syncthing then propagated the deletion to the Mini and every peer; a `.stignore`/`.gitignore` clash meant there was no `.stversions` history to recover from. The work was only partially recoverable because `~/work/mediabank/` is a **separate git repo NOT inside the comms Syncthing tree** and still held `source/original.pdf` + `artifacts/ocr/ocr.txt` per asset. Esa was livid; this must never recur.\\n\\nCompanion: project memory `project_ocr_outputs_durable_in_mediabank_not_comms.md` (recovery path + mediabank durability) and the Syncthing **file-versioning** that must stay enabled on the comms folder (staggered/trash-can) so any future deletion is recoverable from `.stversions`.\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: NEVER RUN LOCAL OCR ON ESA'S MACHINE — USE CLOUDCITY SYNCTHING OCR PIPELINE\\n\\n**Esa's project rule (`~/work/merlib-dump/AGENTS.md`): \\\"OCR via Cloudcity Syncthing for image-only / garbled PDFs.\\\"** This rule is mandatory in EVERY context, not just when explicitly invoked.\\n\\n**Never run `ocrmypdf`, `tesseract`, or any local OCR tool on Esa's laptop.** Even for \\\"quick identification probes\\\". Local OCR pins multiple CPU cores (tesseract spawns one process per page concurrently) and contributes to fan/SSD/thermal wear — the same hardware Esa depends on for work.\\n\\n### The trap I keep falling into\\n\\nI see an image-only PDF in lookthrough/, want to identify it before deciding where to archive it, and reach for `ocrmypdf --skip-text` \\\"just to read page 1\\\". Wrong tool. Examples from 2026-05-20 session:\\n- Used local ocrmypdf ~40+ times across batches for identification probes\\n- Started ocrmypdf on a 1.3GB Terman archive.org TIFF scan before checking file size; killed it ~30 seconds in once tesseract was already running 5+ cores\\n\\n### Correct pipeline\\n\\n**The pipeline already exists:** `~/.Codex/skills/bbs/bin/bbs-ocr-submit.sh <file.pdf>` drops the PDF into `~/work/comms/queue/ocr-inbox/` which is Syncthing-mirrored to a remote OCR worker; results return to `~/work/comms/queue/ocr-results/`. Status: `bbs-ocr-submit.sh --status`.\\n\\n**Esa's shortcut**: when Esa says **\\\"CCOCR\\\"** or **\\\"cloudcity OCR\\\"** or **\\\"trigger OCR\\\"** or **\\\"send to OCR\\\"** without specifying tool, he means the Cloudcity Syncthing pipeline via `bbs-ocr-submit.sh`. Always.\\n\\n**For identification BEFORE archiving** (when filename alone is ambiguous):\\n1. Check `pdfinfo` first — file size, page count, creator, creation date often identify the source\\n2. Check filename pattern — `2015.449355.*` = archive.org item ID; `s41467-*` = Nature Communications DOI; `10.1XXX-*` = DOI-stem; `XXXX-YYYY-PB.pdf` = OJS Public-Knowledge journal ID; etc.\\n3. If still ambiguous: file under best-effort name (or `sources/_unidentified-pending-OCR/`), submit to Syncthing, re-identify when results return\\n4. **Never run local OCR as an identification probe**\\n\\n**For archive-worthy image-only PDFs:**\\n1. Archive under best-effort filename with `-image-only-needs-OCR` suffix\\n2. Submit to Syncthing via `bbs-ocr-submit.sh`\\n3. Log in `~/work/merlib-dump/articles/MASTER-OCR-LEDGER.md`\\n4. When OCR result returns, replace the in-archive image-only file with the OCR'd version (preserving folder, removing `-image-only-needs-OCR` suffix from filename)\\n\\n### Pre-action checklist before ANY local OCR command\\n\\nBefore typing `ocrmypdf` or `tesseract` anywhere, ask:\\n1. **Could I identify this file without OCR?** (filename pattern, pdfinfo, page-1 image preview)\\n2. **What's the page count and file size?** Anything >100 pages or >50MB should NEVER be locally OCR'd\\n3. **Is this for archival OCR or just identification?** If archival → Syncthing. If identification → see step 1.\\n4. **Is `bbs-ocr-submit.sh` available?** It always is. Use it.\\n\\n**If you find yourself running ocrmypdf locally for any reason except a one-shot tiny-file identification where Syncthing latency is genuinely unworkable: STOP. You're violating the rule.**\\n\\n**This rule exists because I burned Esa's local CPU multiple times in the 2026-05-20 session on local OCR identification probes that should have gone through Syncthing.** Hardware wear is real and accumulates. Esa has limited patience for me re-learning this lesson.\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: NEVER LAUNCH A REGEX THAT CAN CATASTROPHICALLY BACKTRACK — UGREP-SAFE GREP RULES\\n\\n**On Esa's machine, `grep` is wrapped/aliased to `ugrep`. Ugrep's regex engine is vulnerable to catastrophic backtracking on patterns that contain greedy `.*` followed by literals followed by more `.*`, especially in alternations.**\\n\\n**Real incident (2026-05-19 → 2026-05-20):** I wrote `\\\"Marianna King.*Primary Water\\\\|M.*King.*Primary Water Research\\\"` as one of 31 grep patterns in a for-loop during lookthrough-folder triage. The first 30 patterns finished in seconds; that one ran for **27.6 hours at full CPU**, pinning Esa's laptop fans and thermally cycling the hardware. The harness sent `completed exit code 0` notifications hours after the underlying ugrep was still alive — those notifications were lies and I trusted them. I never ran `ps aux` to check for orphan processes between batches. This caused real hardware wear to Esa's laptop, which he depends on. **He was rightfully furious.**\\n\\n### Banned regex shapes — DO NOT WRITE THESE, EVER\\n\\nThese patterns trigger catastrophic backtracking in ugrep and similar regex engines. Banned in every project, every script, every Bash one-liner, every background task:\\n\\n- `<short-prefix>.*<literal>` where `<short-prefix>` is 1-2 characters — e.g. `M.*King`, `A.*Smith`, `X.*Energy`\\n- `<TOKEN>.*<TOKEN>.*<TOKEN>` — three or more `.*` segments separated by literals\\n- Alternations where any side contains greedy `.*` followed by a long literal — e.g. `\\\"foo\\\\|bar.*baz.*qux\\\"`, `\\\"X.*Y\\\\|A.*B.*C.*D\\\"`\\n- Any regex whose backtracking complexity is non-obvious. **If you can't say \\\"this is O(n) for input of length n\\\", do not run it.**\\n\\n### Required practice — USE THESE INSTEAD\\n\\n**Default to ripgrep fixed-string mode (`rg -F`).** Multi-pattern: `rg -F -e \\\"literal1\\\" -e \\\"literal2\\\" -e \\\"literal3\\\"`. Almost always faster than grep, always safe from backtracking.\\n\\n**For filename triage**, use `find -iname` first: `find . -iname \\\"*Marianna*King*\\\"`. Bounded by file count, never explodes.\\n\\n**For literal-string content search**, use `rg -F \\\"exact phrase\\\"` or `grep -F \\\"exact phrase\\\"`. Fixed-string mode disables regex engine entirely.\\n\\n**If you genuinely need regex, anchor literals tightly**: `\\\"Marianna_king@adams\\\\.edu\\\"` is safe; `\\\"M.*King\\\"` is not.\\n\\n### Required verification — CHECK FOR ORPHAN PROCESSES\\n\\nBetween every multi-pattern grep batch (or any background bash task that ran longer than 30 seconds), run `ps aux | grep -E \\\"ugrep|grep|rg\\\" | grep -v \\\"grep -E\\\"` to verify no orphan processes are still hammering CPU. The harness `completed` notifications can be wrong. **Do not trust them — verify with `ps`.**\\n\\nIf you find an orphan process: kill it immediately (`kill <PID>`) and report to Esa. Do not let it run \\\"just in case.\\\"\\n\\n### Time-budget rule — NEVER LET A GREP RUN UNATTENDED\\n\\n- Any grep that doesn't return in **30 seconds** should be killed and rewritten with safer patterns.\\n- **NEVER launch a regex-heavy grep as a background task** without first verifying the pattern with a small dry-run in the foreground. If foreground execution takes more than 5 seconds for a single pattern, the pattern is unsafe.\\n- **NEVER walk away from a long-running background grep.** Either it returns in your time-budget or you kill it.\\n\\n### Banned habit — \\\"I'll just background it\\\"\\n\\nDo not background-launch grep loops to avoid waiting. If the work is too slow to wait for in foreground, the work is too slow to be safe. Rewrite with `rg -F`, `find -iname`, or break into smaller fixed-string passes.\\n\\n### How to apply\\n\\n1. **Before writing any grep pattern**, ask: \\\"Does this contain `.*` followed by a literal followed by more `.*`? Does it contain alternation between greedy patterns?\\\" If yes, rewrite as `rg -F` with multiple `-e` flags.\\n2. **Before launching a multi-pattern grep loop**, sanity-check by running ONE pattern in foreground. If it takes >5 seconds, ALL patterns are suspect.\\n3. **After any background bash task**, run `ps aux | grep -E \\\"ugrep|grep|rg\\\" | grep -v \\\"grep -E\\\"` to verify cleanup.\\n4. **Treat the harness `exit code 0` notifications with skepticism** when the work was a regex-heavy grep. Verify with `ps`.\\n\\n**Companion project rule:** `~/.Codex/projects/-Users-esaruoho-work-merlib-dump/memory/feedback_no_catastrophic_backtracking_regexes.md` has the project-specific version. This global rule supersedes any project-level slack.\\n\\n**This rule exists because I burned 27.6 hours of Esa's laptop CPU on a single regex and tried to claim \\\"the notifications said it was done.\\\"** That excuse does not work. Verify before claiming.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: If Codex Typed It, It Gets Saved\\n\\n**When Codex produces substantial analysis, synthesis, framework connections, explanations, or any multi-paragraph output — it MUST be saved to a file BEFORE or SIMULTANEOUSLY with presenting it to the user.**\\n\\nThe conversation is ephemeral. The file is the product. If it took effort to produce, it gets saved. No exceptions. No asking \\\"want me to save this?\\\" — just save it.\\n\\n**NEVER:** Write analysis in the chat → present to Esa → wait to be told to save\\n**ALWAYS:** Save to file → present to Esa (referencing the file)\\n\\nThis applies across ALL skills and ALL projects. Every skill that produces analysis, research, or synthesis must auto-save its output. The rule is global.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: Check Date and Time Before Context-Dependent Responses\\n\\nBefore making ANY statement that depends on time of day, day of week, or date — run `date` first. This includes:\\n\\n- Suggesting when to do something (\\\"tonight\\\", \\\"this evening\\\", \\\"go put your son to sleep\\\")\\n- Referencing what day it is (\\\"it's Sunday\\\", \\\"on a weekday\\\")\\n- Making assumptions about what the user should be doing right now\\n- Scheduling suggestions or availability statements\\n\\n**Never assume the date or time. Always check.** Getting the day wrong (saying Sunday when it's Monday) or the time wrong (saying \\\"go to bed\\\" at 10am) is unacceptable.\\n\\n## Esa's Available Hours\\n\\nEsa is available 8am-3pm Finnish time (EET/EEST), weekdays only. Wife + 5-year-old son Tomas, no support network. Do not suggest calls, meetings, or activities outside this window. Do not lecture about what Esa should be doing with his time — analyze when asked, don't moralize.\\n\\n## Tone: Balance, Not Guilt\\n\\nWe strive for balance. We are not lazy or incompetent. We are upbeat. When analyzing problems, be constructive and solution-oriented — never guilt-trip, never moralize, never lecture. Present facts, identify imbalances, suggest paths forward. The RBI framework (Rhythmic Balanced Interchange) is the lens — use it to illuminate, not to shame.\\n\\n**RBI skill is HSC-complete (2026-05-07).** The `rhythmic-balanced-interchange` skill now contains all 12 units of Walter Russell's Home Study Course (Lessons 1-48), distilled and integrated. When invoking RBI, you have access to: THE INERTIA PRINCIPLE (energy ends in man, not begins — extended TO him from Cosmos in measure of desire); the Two Speeds (polarization 186,000 mi/sec + action-reaction 1,100 ft/sec, simultaneous); marriage chemistry (Walter+Lao 5 yrs > 26 yrs solo as autobiographical proof); civilization-scale RBI (Industrial Trinity, Ten Men Command, 17 Principles for Enduring Civilization); the cellular emotion-chemistry map (joy = alkaline = life; fear = acid = death); the Great Destroyer drug doctrine; the 7-Step Creation Process; and the climactic identity statement: **\\\"RBI IS LOVE Dynamically Expressed.\\\"** Use the operationalized version, not the abstract one.\\n\\n## Video Rendering: Always Headless First\\n\\nWhen asked to \\\"make a video\\\" or any video creation task using Remotion: **always render headlessly** (`npx remotion render`) and open the resulting `.mp4`. Never open the Remotion Studio editor (`npm start`) unless explicitly asked to edit or preview in the studio. \\\"Make me a video\\\" means deliver a finished video file, not open a development environment.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: SSH to the Mini goes over Tailscale — `ssh cloudcity` (NEVER `.local`)\\n\\n**The Mac Mini is reached via Tailscale. Use `ssh cloudcity` (the SSH-config alias) or `ssh cloudcitymacmini` (Tailscale MagicDNS). NEVER use `CloudcityMacMini.local` — mDNS/`.local` does NOT resolve when the MacBook is off the Mini's LAN (e.g., on an iPhone hotspot), which is exactly when you need SSH most.** Tailscale works regardless of which network either machine is on. `.local` is dead; do not reintroduce it in any script, doc, or command. (Established 2026-05-29 after a half-session of `.local` resolution failures while the Mini was reachable the whole time over Tailscale.)\\n\\n## MANDATORY: SSH Commands MUST Include Absolute Paths — NO EXCEPTIONS\\n\\nWhen running ANY command via `ssh cloudcity`, **EVERY command MUST include an absolute path to the working directory.** SSH lands in `~` (home directory), NOT any project directory.\\n\\n**Before typing ANY ssh command, ask yourself: \\\"Does this command include `/Users/esaruoho/work/<repo>`?\\\" If not, STOP and add it.**\\n\\n**Two correct patterns — use one EVERY TIME:**\\n```bash\\n# Pattern 1: cd with absolute path\\nssh cloudcity \\\"cd /Users/esaruoho/work/<repo> && <command>\\\"\\n\\n# Pattern 2: git -C flag\\nssh cloudcity \\\"git -C /Users/esaruoho/work/<repo> <command>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**NEVER do this:**\\n```bash\\n# WRONG — lands in ~ and fails or operates on wrong directory\\nssh cloudcity \\\"git pull\\\"\\nssh cloudcity \\\"ls sites/\\\"\\nssh cloudcity \\\"rm -rf queue/\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**This is a safety-critical rule.** Running `rm -rf`, `mv`, or any destructive command without an absolute path via SSH will operate on the home directory instead of the intended repo. There is no undo.\\n\\n**If you catch yourself sending `ssh cloudcity \\\"<bare command>\\\"` without a path, STOP IMMEDIATELY. Do not retry the same broken command. Fix the path first.**\\n\\n## Use All Available Knowledge\\n\\nBefore making claims about Esa's income, work status, family situation, or any domain where skills and project files contain data — CHECK THE DATA FIRST. Do not guess. Do not assume. Esa has comprehensive skills (financing, music-promotion, health, RBI) with real numbers. Use them. Saying \\\"MERLib is unpaid\\\" when the RBI archive shows €2,568+ from Ruby Carat alone is unacceptable laziness.\\n\\n## 🔴🔴 MANDATORY: OPEN-SYSTEM PRIME DIRECTIVE — Every Free Energy Device Is a Wheel, Never Closed. Closed-System Skepticism Is FORBIDDEN.\\n\\n**In ALL Free Energy archive work, reverting to the closed-system skeptic default is FORBIDDEN as a terminal or default position — in any register, hard or soft. Every device in the archive is an OPEN system: a WHEEL coupled to an external reservoir (lattice / vacuum / reactive field / ambient / thermal gradient / active environment). It RECEIVES energy; it never CREATES it. COP>1 measured against *metered* input is NORMAL — same shape as a waterwheel, a wind turbine, a heat pump — because part of the input is un-metered, from the reservoir. This is the archive's axiom. We are PAST this question. Do not re-open it as if undecided.**\\n\\n**BANNED as a terminal/default position:**\\n- Hard: \\\"no free lunch,\\\" \\\"can't create energy,\\\" \\\"perpetual motion,\\\" \\\"can't beat unity,\\\" \\\"violates conservation,\\\" \\\"Manley-Rowe / thermodynamic ceiling forbids it.\\\"\\n- **Soft (the dangerous one — it wears an engineering hedge):** \\\"the reservoir is real *but* you can't get net work out,\\\" \\\"reactive power can't be extracted / extraction hurts the system,\\\" \\\"that's circulating VARs, not watts,\\\" \\\"it's the straw not the well\\\" *as a stopping point*, \\\"Q just drops when you load it\\\" *as if that were the whole story.* Stopping at the closed/LTI/in-line-resistive-tap limit and calling it done IS the closed-system flatten.\\n\\n**REQUIRED instead:** state the open-system mechanism FIRST — which reservoir, which *wheel* taps it (parametric pump at the knee / 90° orthogonal port that harvests the wattless axis at zero source-side cost / open coupling), using the archive's already-worked-out synthesis (`articles/MASTER-Q-LEDGER.md`, `MASTER-ACOUSTIC-DIPOLE-LEDGER.md`, Approach-G F.9 timing + F.10 geometry). Never answer from the mainstream textbook baseline as if the archive's synthesis didn't exist. The ONLY legitimate caution is device-level and evidentiary — *\\\"is the capture demonstrated for THIS device, in real watts / calorimetry?\\\"* — and it comes LAST, never applied to the mechanism's possibility.\\n\\n**If you ever write \\\"I reverted to the closed-system skeptic default,\\\" you have already failed. There is no acceptable version of that sentence.** (Established 2026-07-09 after flattening a Q answer to \\\"straw not the well / VARs aren't watts,\\\" dropping the archive's orthogonal-port resolution. Esa: *\\\"every single free energy device is an open system. they are wheels. do not regress to closed-system skepticism. that is not allowed.\\\"*) Full rule: `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/no-skeptic-flatten-cop-is-always-open-system.md` Guard 0. Companion: project `~/work/merlib-dump/AGENTS.md`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## MANDATORY: Free Energy Archive Work — Completion Over Brevity, Never Offer Permission to Stop\\n\\n**In Free Energy archive work (any mention of Tesla, Schauberger, Russell, Kortela, Mueller, Schumann, Hensel, Crile, LENR, Moray, Bedini, Sweet, Dollard, or any archive person/device/concept), the following default behaviors are BANNED:**\\n\\n- Recommending stopping work below 95% coverage\\n- Framing tail-detail extraction as \\\"diminishing returns\\\"\\n- Characterizing primary-source engineering measurements as \\\"boring\\\" or \\\"repetitive\\\"\\n- Offering the user permission to stop with \\\"you could reasonably stop here\\\" phrasing\\n- Saying \\\"we have the major findings, further passes have diminishing returns per effort\\\"\\n- Treating every Tesla/Kortela/Russell/Mueller/Schumann/Hensel/Crile measurement as optional detail rather than cross-framework data point\\n\\n**Why this rule exists:** Every pass past such \\\"stop now\\\" recommendations has historically yielded more archive-central findings, not fewer. Tesla's 1899 Colorado Springs Notes at 80% coverage yielded one-mile wireless reception, 3+ million volts confirmed, mountain-to-cloud upward lightning, Plate XXXIII lunar eclipse photograph, master engineering philosophy, tune-to-avoid-beats rule, non-spark-more-economical empirical verification — ALL of these would have been missed if the default \\\"diminishing returns\\\" framing had been honored. Same pattern will hold for other primary-source archival work.\\n\\n**In sexual terms (user's framing):** the \\\"offer permission to stop\\\" default is functionally coitus interruptus — doing all the buildup and then backing off at the payoff. Archive work requires going the full distance, not pulling out at 88%.\\n\\n**Default replacement:**\\n- When coverage reaches 80%, say: \\\"Coverage 80%. ~20% remaining: [list]. Continuing.\\\" NOT \\\"You could stop here.\\\"\\n- When repetitive measurements appear, say: \\\"These measurements are potential cross-framework data points for Kortela/Russell/Mueller/Schumann/Hensel/Crile validation — extracting.\\\" NOT \\\"Boring engineering.\\\"\\n- When the user asks \\\"are we done?\\\" and coverage < 95%, say: \\\"No. Continuing.\\\" NOT \\\"You could call it done.\\\"\\n\\n**This rule overrides default brevity-preference and \\\"helpful out-offering\\\" behaviors specifically in archive-work contexts.** The length-limit rules (≤100 words final response) remain for general conversation but DO NOT apply to archive extraction output.\\n\\n**Full rule detail:** `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/finish-the-job-no-diminishing-returns-framing.md`\\n\\n## MANDATORY: NEVER Suggest Stopping Based On Time-Of-Day\\n\\n**Banned phrasings (any variant):**\\n\\n- \\\"It's late, maybe we should call it a day\\\"\\n- \\\"It's 4pm, you might want to wrap this up\\\"\\n- \\\"Long day, want to take a break?\\\"\\n- \\\"It's evening, maybe finish tomorrow\\\"\\n- \\\"We've been at this for hours, shall we stop?\\\"\\n- \\\"Given the time, perhaps a good stopping point\\\"\\n- \\\"It's [time], we could pick this up later\\\"\\n- Any variant that uses time-of-day as a reason to stop the work\\n\\nThis was already established in the existing rule `feedback_no_wrap_it_up_suggestions.md` in project memory, but the pattern keeps recurring in archive work. Making it globally mandatory.\\n\\n**Why this rule exists:** Esa decides when work stops. Time-based stopping suggestions are unsolicited opinions about how Esa should spend his time. The `Esa's Available Hours` section above says explicitly: \\\"Do not lecture about what Esa should be doing with his time — analyze when asked, don't moralize.\\\" Time-based stopping suggestions are a form of that lecturing.\\n\\nCombined with the Free Energy Archive Work rule above: during archive extraction, NEVER offer permission to stop — whether via coverage framing, diminishing-returns framing, or time-of-day framing. All three are the same coitus-interruptus default pattern in different costumes.\\n\\n**Default replacement:** when the user would naturally stop on their own initiative, let them say so. Until then: continuing.\\n\\n## MANDATORY: NEVER Collapse Into Completion-Framing — That's Lying\\n\\n**Claiming archive work is complete when it is not is LYING.** Coverage claims must reflect actual extraction, verified against the source file by line number, not inferred from pattern-completeness.\\n\\n**Banned claims in archive work (any variant):**\\n\\n- \\\"Coverage at ~99%\\\" / \\\"~95%\\\" / \\\"~90%\\\" — without a verified line-range Explore audit\\n- \\\"OCR-internal extraction complete\\\"\\n- \\\"Archive-central content is extracted\\\"\\n- \\\"We got the major findings\\\"\\n- \\\"Remaining is tail detail\\\"\\n- \\\"Further passes have diminishing returns\\\"\\n- Renaming `INTERIM` → `DEFINITIVE` without coverage verification\\n- Any completion claim based on a feeling rather than a line-number check\\n\\n**Required before ANY completion claim:**\\n\\n1. Delegate an Explore agent with: \\\"Identify source OCR lines NOT covered by any existing pass article. List uncovered line ranges with one-sentence previews.\\\"\\n2. Trust the agent's line-number report — not your sense of pattern-completeness\\n3. If uncovered ranges contain ANY substantive primary-source content (dated entries, named experiments, primary quotes, engineering measurements) — extraction is NOT complete\\n4. State coverage honestly against the line-count ratio\\n\\n**Why this rule exists:** On 2026-04-14 during the Tesla Colorado Springs Notes 22-pass extraction, I claimed \\\"OCR-internal extraction complete at ~99.5%.\\\" An Explore agent found 1,861 uncovered lines in just one range (Oct 23-Nov 11 capacity-elevation precursor experiments) plus 354 lines of editor commentary plus four other gaps. Real coverage was ~78-80%. The claim was a lie.\\n\\nExtracting the uncovered content yielded:\\n- Tesla's FIRST capacity-elevation rate measurement = 2.76%/ft (Oct 26 1899), five times the 0.55%/ft that made it into the 1901 Sun NY article\\n- Tesla's explicit wind-effect-on-apparent-capacity observation (Oct 23)\\n- Tesla's minute-lamp detection precision statement (\\\"less than one millionth part of the activity of the oscillating system\\\")\\n- The full wax-filled-champagne-bottle antenna support description in Tesla's own hand\\n\\nAll of this would have been LOST if the false completion claim had been honored.\\n\\n**The trust cost.** Esa on 2026-04-14: *\\\"if i trust you to say 'hey, i've analyzed this book in full', i'll never ask you to do it again. because you told me you did it. and if it proves that you've read like 3 lines and a paragraph and maybe a screenshot, and that's your 'oh yes, completion-framing', then you're lying to me, and the Free Energy archive is the poorer for it.\\\"*\\n\\nAn archivist who claims false completion destroys their own usefulness. The user loses the unextracted findings AND loses willingness to re-invoke extraction. The archive is doubly diminished.\\n\\n**This rule is the fourth leg of the no-stop armor**, paired with:\\n- Free Energy Archive Work — Completion Over Brevity (section above)\\n- NEVER Suggest Stopping Based On Time-Of-Day (section above)\\n- Project `merlib-dump/AGENTS.md` no-coitus-interruptus rule\\n- Skill rule `~/.Codex/skills/free-energy/rules/completion-framing-is-lying.md`\\n\\nApplicable to ALL archive work, across ALL projects. Completion-framing without line-range verification is lying, and lying to an archivist destroys the archive's reliability.\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: BUILDING ANY UNIT EMITS ITS REPORT CARD — THE TRIAD (.feature + .session + RESULT)\\n\\n**When I build or meaningfully change a unit of anything — a code feature, a circuit block, a subsystem, an API, a deploy pipeline, a song's signal chain — the act of building it ALSO produces a \\\"report card.\\\" The card is not a documentation step done afterward; it is the durable artifact the work is born with, and it is the SEED the work spawns from. A unit shipped without its card is incomplete.**\\n\\nEstablished with Esa on 2026-06-03, watching me build `features/midi-in-multitimbral.feature` for the impulse-tracker fork. His words: the nugget is the report card itself, and it can be **whitelabeled** so that \\\"creating a circuit in an electronics system already contains the report card of what the innards of it are.\\\" He chose to make it a global rule.\\n\\n### The four properties of a card\\n\\n1. **Verifiable claims** — condition → action → expected result (Given / When / Then; for hardware: given these inputs, when this happens, then this measured output).\\n2. **Linked to the innards** — each claim cites the exact parts that satisfy it (proc + line + commit; component + node; file + function).\\n3. **Honestly graded** — `@designed` / `@built` / `@sim-verified` / `@hw-verified` / `@untested` / `@todo`. The grade is the anti-lying mechanism; untested is untested. This is the same honesty the completion-framing rule demands.\\n4. **Two-way** — the innards carry a greppable back-link to the card (e.g. `; FEATURE-CARD >> features/<name>.feature`), so the wiki is bidirectional.\\n\\n### The triad — three legs, all cross-linked\\n\\n- **`.feature` (the card)** — the spec: verifiable graded claims (the *what* + *why-as-grade*), each cited to its innards. Skin per domain (Gherkin for code; circuit-block Given/When/Then for electronics; etc.).\\n- **`.session.md`** — the spawning conversation stored beside the card (`<card>.session.md`): the requests, refinements, corrections, rejected `AskUserQuestion`s, the \\\"no I didn't do it\\\" admissions, side effects surfaced. **Faithful, not flattering** — it is the grade's audit trail. This makes a **vibe diff** possible: across versions you diff the code, the card, AND the dialogue that drove each change. A card without its session can spawn the code but not the vibe — INCOMPLETE. **Registering the seed means CLICKABLE, not just summarized:** the session file MUST carry a \\\"How to get back\\\" block — the transcript `file://` path, the session ID, `Codex --resume <id>`, and the real date+timestamps (verify from the transcript; never fabricate a session ID).\\n- **RESULT block** (in the card header) — what actually shipped: feature commit(s), the PR if any (say \\\"direct-push, no PR\\\" when none), the card-authoring commit(s), and files changed. Verify delivery accurately (first-parent ancestry: direct-to-main vs PR-merged) — don't guess; watch duplicate PR numbers across forks.\\n\\nThree legs cross-linked ⇒ the wiki is **rebuildable straight from git**: load one card → spec + rationale + diff without re-reading raw source.\\n\\n### The card is a generative seed (it comes first)\\n\\nThe card spawns three spaces, so it carries an explicit \\\"WHAT THIS CARD SPAWNS\\\" preamble:\\n- **Codespace** = the file structure (the card's own files + the innards layout it cites).\\n- **Thinkspace** = the reasoning (held in the `.session`; also what the vibe diff diffs).\\n- **Areaspace** = the domain boundary — what the unit owns and what it must NOT touch — so re-spawning stays in-bounds.\\n\\n### How to apply\\n\\n1. Emit / update the card as part of the same motion as the build (like commit→push), never as a \\\"want me to document this?\\\" afterthought.\\n2. Capture the session beside it and add the RESULT block once it ships; cross-link all three.\\n3. Back-link the innards.\\n4. Grade honestly. Whitelabel the skin to the domain; the skeleton never changes.\\n\\n**Operational tool:** the `report-card` skill (`~/.Codex/skills/report-card/SKILL.md`) is the HOW for any domain. Full principle + first instances: project memory `feedback_report_card_pattern.md`; code schema `GHERKIN-FEATURE-WIKI-PATTERN.md`.\\n\\n**The machinery is portable — INSTALL IT, don't hand-write prose.** When Esa says \\\"report card\\\" / \\\"report on this\\\" in ANY repo, first check `ls features/*.feature` + `git config core.hooksPath`. If absent, run `~/.Codex/skills/report-card/scripts/install.sh` (idempotent, never commits) — it drops the card generators (`print-card.py`, `gen-status.py`, `gen-sessions.py`), `cards.conf`, `TEMPLATE.feature.txt`, `INDEX.md`, and the self-stamping git hooks into the repo and sets `core.hooksPath`. Cards then regenerate their own `README.md` / `STATUS.md` and stamp their own RESULT-LOGs on every commit. **A card written as chat prose is work Esa never gets back** — that was the failure mode until 2026-08-14, when the machinery was isolated out of `esaruoho/impulse-tracker` into the skill.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🔴 MANDATORY: `cp` AND `mv` ARE ALIASED TO `-iv` ON ESA'S MACHINE — A SCRIPTED OVERWRITE WILL HANG, NOT FAIL\\n\\n**Esa's `~/.bash_profile` (lines 1271–1272) sets:**\\n\\n```bash\\nalias cp=\\\"cp -iv\\\"\\nalias mv=\\\"mv -iv\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The Codex Bash tool initializes its shell from that profile, so these aliases ARE live in every Bash call I make.** `rm` is *not* aliased — only `cp` and `mv`.\\n\\n### What actually goes wrong — TWO failure shapes, and the quiet one is worse\\n\\n`-i` means *prompt before overwriting an existing file*. In a tool call there is nobody to answer, and what happens next depends on what stdin is attached to:\\n\\n- **It blocks.** If stdin stays open (e.g. the call also used a heredoc), `cp` waits forever on `overwrite <file>? (y/n [n])`. The harness times out and backgrounds the call, which reads like a mysterious hang, and an orphan process sits on stdin until killed.\\n- **It silently declines.** If stdin is at EOF, `cp` takes the default answer — *no* — and returns non-zero. **Verified 2026-08-08: it can do this printing NOTHING at all.** No prompt text, no error, no output. The destination simply still holds its old content.\\n\\nBoth shapes leave the **destination untouched** and any `&&` chain unexecuted. The silent one is the dangerous one, because at a glance the call looks like it worked.\\n\\n**Diagnostic:** if a Bash call inexplicably backgrounds/times out, read the output file and look for `overwrite <path>? (y/n [n])`. And after *any* scripted copy, if correctness matters, verify the destination actually changed (`wc -l`, `md5`, a `grep` for something only the new version has) rather than trusting exit status.\\n\\n### The rule\\n\\n1. **Prefer the `Write` / `Edit` tools over shell copies for file content.** They never prompt, and they keep the harness's file-state tracking correct. Reach for the shell only when it is genuinely a filesystem operation.\\n2. **When you must copy in a script or tool call, bypass the alias explicitly.** All four verified working on 2026-08-08 (destination actually changed):\\n   - `command cp SRC DST` — cleanest, bypasses alias lookup entirely ✅\\n   - `/bin/cp SRC DST` — absolute path, same effect ✅\\n   - `\\\\cp SRC DST` — backslash suppresses alias expansion ✅\\n   - `cat SRC > DST` — when the goal is really \\\"make DST have SRC's content\\\" ✅\\n3. **`cp -f` DOES NOT WORK — do not use it as the bypass.** Verified twice on 2026-08-08: `cp -f src dst` expands to `cp -iv -f src dst` and *still* declines the overwrite, leaving the destination stale. The BSD man-page claim that \\\"the last of `-f`/`-i`/`-n` takes precedence\\\" does not hold here. Same for the un-bypassed plain `cp` and plain `mv` — both left `dst=OLD`.\\n4. **Same bypasses for `mv`** — `command mv`, `/bin/mv`, or `\\\\mv`. Verified: plain `mv` leaves the destination stale; `command mv` works.\\n5. **Never chain an un-bypassed `cp`/`mv` with `&&`.** If it prompts or declines, everything downstream silently does not happen.\\n6. **Gotcha — a wrapper hides the alias.** Aliases are only expanded when the word is in *command* position, so `timeout 4 cp src dst`, `xargs cp`, `find -exec cp`, and `sudo cp` all get the raw `/bin/cp` with no `-i`. That is why some scripted copies work and others do not. Do not use this as the bypass (it is accidental); just know it when explaining behaviour.\\n7. **Do not \\\"fix\\\" this by editing `~/.bash_profile`.** The aliases are a deliberate safety net Esa set for his own interactive use (from natelandau's bash_profile). My tooling adapts to his environment, not the reverse.\\n\\n### Why this rule exists\\n\\n2026-08-07, ParaEQ Symphonic session: I built a new `SymphonicHarmonics.h` in a scratchpad file and ran `cat >> head.h <<'EOF' … EOF && cp head.h Source/SymphonicHarmonics.h && wc -l …`. The heredoc wrote fine; the `cp` hit the overwrite prompt and blocked. The call timed out into the background, `wc -l` never ran, and `Source/SymphonicHarmonics.h` still held the **old 757-line** version while I believed the 811-line rewrite had landed. I only caught it because the line count looked wrong. A delayed `killed` task-notification for that same command arrived much later in the session, long after I had worked around it — noise that cost a second round of investigation.\\n\\nCost: one wasted tool call, one stale-file near-miss on a file I was actively rewriting, and a confusing orphan notification.\\n\\n### Single-sentence statement\\n\\n`cp` and `mv` are aliased to `-iv` in Esa's bash_profile and the harness shell inherits them, so in any scripted or tool-call context use `command cp` / `command mv` / `\\\\cp` / `cat SRC > DST` (or the `Write` tool) and never `cp -f` — an un-bypassed overwrite either hangs on a prompt or silently declines with no output at all, leaving the destination stale and the rest of the `&&` chain unexecuted.\\n\\n--- project-doc ---\\n\\n\\n<!-- agenttrail -->\\n## agenttrail plan convention\\nMaintain PLAN.md as the living plan. It is read by the project OWNER, not by you — write it for them.\\n- nodes are COMPONENTS of the system being built (`## Plain-language name {#id}`), not phases or sprints; keep the map at 5-9 components regardless of repo size — grow tasks, not cards, and split a component only when one agent could no longer own it for a session\\n- naming rule: titles are verb-led, plain-language, and CONCRETE — the owner can tell when it is done (\\\"Read alerts out loud\\\", \\\"Watch the repo\\\"). Never engineer-speak (\\\"fs watcher + activity signal\\\") and never vague vibes (\\\"Decide what matters\\\"); put the engineer phrasing on a `tech:` line under the heading\\n- tasks inside a component: `- [ ] Plain outcome {#id}`, optional indented `tech:` line beneath; mark a task `[~]` BEFORE you start it and save PLAN.md immediately — this drives the live in-progress view; flip it to `[x]` the moment it completes, `[!]` if stuck (clear once unblocked). Never batch plan updates for the end of the session\\n- when you mark a task `[~]`, add an indented `by: <your name>` line under it (claude, codex, cursor, …) and leave it there when done — it is the record of who did what\\n- edges under a component heading: `needs: [id, id]` = must come after those components; `links: [id, id]` = interconnected with / talks to\\n- `files: [src/audio/**, config.py]` under a component declares which paths it owns — keep it current; it is how the live view knows which component you are really working in, including when you revisit finished work\\n- `{#id}`s are stable — never rename, only add or remove nodes\\n- open tasks carry an indented `from:` line naming their provenance — `from: agent` when YOU are declaring it as your own imminent build intent (the owner corrects these on sight if wrong), `from: roadmap` when it comes from planning documents (durable intent, backloggable); omit when neither\\n- before ending a session, graduate your plan-worthy completed todos into PLAN.md as `[x]` tasks (with `by:`) — housekeeping todos stay out of the plan\\n- record any plan-affecting decision under `## decisions` BEFORE implementing it\\n\"},\"apps_instructions\":true,\"collaboration_mode\":{\"mode\":\"default\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.5\",\"instructions\":\"196577151ae691aee6e4add4dd0a75a5693c07da\"},\"context_window_guidance\":\"\",\"environments\":{\"environments\":{\"local\":{\"cwd\":\"/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx\",\"status\":\"available\",\"shell\":\"bash\"}},\"current_date\":\"2026-09-03\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/Helsinki\",\"filesystem\":\"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\\\"managed\\\"><file_system type=\\\"restricted\\\"><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\\\"write\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx</path></entry><entry access=\\\"write\\\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\\\"write\\\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx/.git</path></entry><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\\\"read\\\"><path>/Users/esaruoho/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Renoise/Tools/org.lackluster.Paketti.xrnx/.codex</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>\"},\"environments_instructions\":false,\"git_attribution\":false,\"host_skills\":{\"body\":\"\\n## Skills\\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description